/* THE STAR IS PLACED FROM THE USABLE BOX (phone review, 23 Nov 2026). The
   body runs under the iPhone Duo's status column, so 32px from the body's
   right edge put the star half under the clock: a pixelated stain, not a
   star. With the column on the right it stands that much further in. */
html[data-side-strip="right"] body { --home-star-x: calc(32px + var(--platform-strip-w, 0px)); }
/* AND A PHONE'S STANDARD LOOK DRAWS NONE. Its 78px were placed for a 52px
   bar at the top of the screen. Under a phone's status bar all but a sliver
   is behind the system's own clock and battery, and once the search row is
   pulled down the rest lands behind New project, where it read as a smudge.
   `none` is a valid layer image, so the shorthands above keep their shape;
   !important because the dark rule above is the more specific selector. */
@media (max-width: 767.98px) {
  html:root body { --home-star: none !important; }
}
/* THE HEADER STAYS CLEAR OF THE STAR in a running app and on New project
   (polish N20). On Home the star sits behind the bell by design, but over
   somebody's app the bar is the app's header, and on the make screen the
   bar holds only the ✕ and the mark: there it read as a stray shape showing
   through. Both draw the same wallpaper with its first layer, the star,
   left out. `html:root` lifts this past the dark and wide rules above,
   which define the full shorthand at the same route test. The make screen
   paints `var(--home-wallpaper)` on its own root (make.tsx), so the
   variable is redefined there too; it reads the body's grain and washes. */
html:root body:has(#app-view:not(.hidden):not([data-app-surface="platform"])),
[data-first-session-make] {
  --home-wallpaper:
    var(--home-grain) 0 0 / 240px 240px repeat,
    var(--home-washes);
}
/* THE BAR OVER THE WALLPAPER. On every wallpaper route the header's
   zinc-200 surface goes, so the wallpaper (and the star) run up behind the
   bell — the one place the design's "page ground runs to the top of the
   screen" is literal. (#landing-header never had a surface; it sits on
   the body's ground already.) The controls on it carry their own
   periwinkle fill and hairline (--brand-tint / --brand-line), so they
   still lift off cream and off dark alike. Everywhere else the bar keeps
   its surface — see the "THE BAR'S OWN SURFACE" note in
   platform-header.tsx for why it has one. The view-transition group note
   above (#platform-header is opaque) has its exception here, and it is
   what makes the launcher → app push read as one bar: both routes paint
   the same wallpaper under the same cleared bar, so the bar's snapshot is
   identical on both sides of the transition and only the page moves. */
body:has(:is(#home-screen, #app-view, #leaderboard-screen, #profile-screen, #settings-screen, #messages-screen, #workshop-screen, #weekly-digest-screen, #communities-screen, #ai-screen, #profile-proposals-screen, #auth-landing-screen, #auth-waiting-screen, #auth-waitlist-screen, #auth-more-screen):not(.hidden)) #platform-header {
  background-color: transparent;
}

/* ── THE BAR IS THE SAME MATERIAL AS THE BAR AT THE FOOT (#2718 review) ──

   The rule above clears the header on every screen that paints the
   wallpaper, so the ground runs straight through it. That was right while
   the bar floated alone on a page; it is wrong now that a second bar is on
   screen with it, because the two are the same KIND of thing — the platform's
   own chrome around somebody's content — and they were drawn as two
   different ones: `.platform-tabs` a frosted wall with a hairline, the header
   nothing at all.

   So the header takes the tab bar's surface exactly: `--dc-sheet-fill` under
   `--dc-frost`, the pane material every floating surface in the shell wears.
   On a desktop the rail and the header meet at the corner and read as one
   continuous edge around the page, which is what the design draws.

   NOT INSIDE AN APP, and not signed out. `#app-view` keeps the clear bar
   because there is no tab bar there to match and the strip takes the APP's
   tone instead (`data-app-tone`, #1945) — a frost over somebody else's page
   colour is a smear, not a surface. The auth screens have no bar either.

   NO BORDER-BOTTOM. The tab bar's hairline is its top edge meeting the page;
   this bar's bottom edge is `rounded-b-2xl -mb-2`, a notch every platform
   surface below tucks 8px up into, so a line drawn across it would cut the
   notch in half. The frost IS the edge here. */
body:has(:is(#home-screen, #leaderboard-screen, #profile-screen,
             #settings-screen, #messages-screen, #workshop-screen, #weekly-digest-screen, #communities-screen, #ai-screen,
             #profile-proposals-screen):not(.hidden)) #platform-header {
  background-color: var(--dc-sheet-fill);
  backdrop-filter: var(--dc-frost);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: var(--dc-frost);
}
/* …AND OVER AN APP'S OWN WORKSHOP (#2806). The exclusion above is about the
   APP: its page colour is under the bar, and the bar takes that tone instead.
   An app's Workshop tab (board, sessions, a discussion) is not the app, it is
   the platform's own page about it — `data-app-surface="platform"`, set by
   AppView._setSurface for everything the shell renders into #app-content —
   and with the frame parked there is no app tone either (toneForState answers
   null). So it is the same chrome as the standalone Workshop screen and wears
   the same glass; left clear, the bar went bare on exactly those pages and
   nowhere else. A separate rule, not a widened selector: the one above is
   pinned to exclude #app-view, because a running app must stay clear. */
body:has(#app-view:not(.hidden)[data-app-surface="platform"]) #platform-header {
  background-color: var(--dc-sheet-fill);
  backdrop-filter: var(--dc-frost);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: var(--dc-frost);
}
/* …AND THE HEADER, faked the same way as the tab bar ("FAKED GLASS" below;
   its layer is in the shared rule there),
   on exactly the routes the glass rules above cover. The clip follows the
   bar's own `rounded-b-2xl` (1.25rem in tailwind.config.js), so the notch
   the surfaces below tuck into keeps its shape. Nothing inside the header
   reaches past its box, so the clip cuts nothing. */
body:has(:is(#home-screen, #leaderboard-screen, #profile-screen,
             #settings-screen, #messages-screen, #workshop-screen, #weekly-digest-screen, #communities-screen, #ai-screen,
             #profile-proposals-screen):not(.hidden)) #platform-header,
body:has(#app-view:not(.hidden)[data-app-surface="platform"]) #platform-header {
  background-color: transparent;
  clip-path: inset(0 round 0 0 1.25rem 1.25rem);
}
/* THE STRIP OVER THE BAR FOLLOWS IT HOME (onboarding run three, 12 Oct
   2026, iPhone 17 Safari 26). In a browser tab Safari colours the strip
   behind the status bar from the fixed bar's own `background-color`, and
   only an opaque one: the project's green bar turned it green, and back on
   Home, whose bar is transparent with its glass drawn by the layer below,
   it stayed green (measured on a copy: green, then transparent, held the
   green; an opaque colour took it back at once). So the bar here has an
   opaque ground under its glass, the glass's own solid plane colour. The
   layer covers it whole (its washes end on the ground colour), so nothing
   on the bar changes; only the strip does.
   A bar whose ground is a colour of its own names it in
   `--platform-header-solid`: the phone's blue bar does ("THE PHONE'S TOP BAR
   IS THE TAB BAR'S BLUE"), so the strip turns blue with it. */
html.un-ios.web-browser-chrome:not([data-community-tint]) body:has(:is(#home-screen, #leaderboard-screen, #profile-screen,
             #settings-screen, #messages-screen, #workshop-screen, #weekly-digest-screen, #communities-screen, #ai-screen,
             #profile-proposals-screen):not(.hidden)) #platform-header,
html.un-ios.web-browser-chrome:not([data-community-tint]) body:has(#app-view:not(.hidden)[data-app-surface="platform"]) #platform-header {
  background-color: var(--platform-header-solid, var(--dc-sheet-solid));
}
/* …AND ON THE APP TAB, WHERE THE PAGE IS NOT THE SCROLLER (newcomer run
   six, 14 Oct 2026, iPhone Safari 26). Safari takes the strip's colour from
   a bar FIXED (or sticky) at the top. On the routes above a phone browser
   scrolls the document (lib/browser-scroll.ts), so the bar is sticky
   (`html[data-browser-scroller]`) and the strip follows its ground. The App
   tab of a first version being built is the platform's own page, but the
   document is not its scroller (pageScroller answers only the project's own
   pages, #dev-forum-scroll), so its bar sat in the flow, Safari found no bar
   to take a colour from, and the strip stayed the hub's teal over the App
   tab's light bar. Sticky at the top here as well: the page does not
   scroll, so the bar stays exactly where it was, and the strip takes the
   opaque ground the rule above gives it. Back on the hub the bar is sticky
   and tinted again (`data-community-tint`), and the strip turns teal.
   …AND ON EVERY OTHER SCREEN THE DOCUMENT DOES NOT SCROLL (newcomer run
   seven, 14 Oct 2026, iPhone Safari 26): from the hub to Messages the strip
   stayed teal over Messages' light bar, for the same reason. Of the routes
   above, only Home, Browse, Leaderboard, Profile and Settings are page
   scrollers (lib/browser-scroll.ts PAGES); Messages, Communities, #general,
   an agent's session and a profile's proposals each scroll an element of
   their own, so their bar is sticky here too. Sticky changes no layout on
   them: it offsets the bar only when its nearest scroller, <body>
   (`overflow: hidden`, 100dvh), scrolls, and nothing scrolls it, so the
   bar stays where it sits in the flow; and it is already `relative z-10`,
   so it was a stacking context and a containing block before. */
html.un-ios.web-browser-chrome:not([data-community-tint]):not([data-browser-scroller]) body:has(:is(#messages-screen, #workshop-screen, #weekly-digest-screen, #communities-screen, #ai-screen,
             #profile-proposals-screen):not(.hidden)) #platform-header,
html.un-ios.web-browser-chrome:not([data-community-tint]):not([data-browser-scroller]) body:has(#app-view:not(.hidden)[data-app-surface="platform"]) #platform-header {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
}
/* THE BAR TAKES THE APP'S TONE (#1945). A dark app under the light shell
   used to sit beneath a cream strip, and a light app under the dark shell
   beneath a night one: the bar has no surface inside an app, so the
   shell's wallpaper ran up behind its controls whatever colour the page
   under them was. The framed app's bridge already tells the shell its
   opaque page colour (`__usernode_background`, #1581);
   features/app-frame/app-tone.js reads it as a tone and writes
   `data-app-tone` on <html> while the frame is on screen. The wallpaper
   rules above key off it, and these carry the tokens that have to move
   with the ground: the bar's periwinkle controls (--brand-*: the chip, the
   bell, the back disc, which "lift off cream and off dark alike" only with
   the value for the ground they are on) and the sheet edge (--app-sheet-*:
   a black hairline is invisible on a night ground, a white one on cream).

   SCOPED TO THE BAR AND THE SHEET, deliberately, rather than to <html>.
   The theme is still the shell's: `.dark` on <html> is the theme module's
   alone and every `dark:` utility keeps reading it. The sheets, dialogs and
   menus opened over a dark app follow its tone too since #2803, but by
   carrying `.dark` themselves (lib/surface-tone.ts, and the
   `.platform-app-tone-dark` rule below), not through these tokens. Custom properties
   inherit, so setting them on the two elements reaches every control and
   edge inside them and nothing outside. The chip's subtitle is the one
   control drawn with a `dark:` utility rather than a brand token, so it is
   re-inked here by hand.

   THE INK AND THE SURFACES MOVE WITH THE GROUND TOO (#2704) — and that is
   what these blocks were missing. The ground is repainted PAGE-WIDE (the
   wallpaper rules above put --home-ground at #0b0d1b for a dark app under
   the light shell), but the tokens were scoped here and carried only the
   brand controls and the sheet edge. So everything inside the bar and the
   sheet that inks itself from the platform's text ramp, or paints itself
   from its surface tokens, kept the LIGHT values on a ground that had
   already gone dark. That is the hybrid: a dark page with light-theme parts
   on it, which is exactly as readable as it sounds.

   THE LAUNCH COVER IS WHERE IT WAS WORST, and it is on screen every time an
   app opens. .app-launch-cover takes --home-ground (the rule above) and
   .app-launch-cover-name takes --text-primary, so the app's own name was
   painted #0a0a0a on #0b0d1b: 1.03:1, which is not dim, it is gone. The
   mirror case is identical — a light app under the dark shell put #f5f5f7
   on cream, also 1.03:1. Carrying the ramp takes both to 17.6:1, which is
   the contrast the same cover already had in the untoned theme.

   THE VALUES ARE THE THEME'S OWN, dark tone from `.dark` and light tone from
   `:root`, so a toned surface stays byte-identical to a themed one — the
   invariant tests/app-tone.test.js already pins for the brand tokens.
   --text-faint and --text-muted are one value in both themes today and are
   carried anyway, so the ramp is complete and a later divergence cannot
   quietly reopen the split.

   STILL SCOPED TO THE BAR AND THE SHEET, and still never to <html>. This
   widens what the strip repaints; it does not widen what the strip is. The
   floating surfaces are the next block's job. */
[data-app-tone="dark"]:not(.dark) #platform-header,
[data-app-tone="dark"]:not(.dark) #app-frame-host {
  color: #eaeaea; /* zinc-100, the body's dark ink */
  --text-primary: #f5f5f7;
  --text-secondary: #e3e3e6;
  --text-muted: #98989d;
  --text-faint: #8e8e93;
  --bg-primary: #0b0b0c;
  --bg-secondary: #1c1c1e;
  --border: #2c2c2e;
  --border-light: #3a3a3c;
  --brand-ink: #c7d0f5;
  --brand-tint: #262b45;
  --brand-line: #3a4270;
  --app-sheet-line: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12);
  --app-sheet-shadow-near: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55);
  --app-sheet-shadow-far: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4);
}
.dark[data-app-tone="light"] #platform-header,
.dark[data-app-tone="light"] #app-frame-host {
  color: #1c1c1e; /* zinc-900, the body's light ink */
  --text-primary: #0a0a0a;
  --text-secondary: #3a3a3c;
  --text-muted: #68686c;
  --text-faint: #8e8e93;
  --bg-primary: #ffffff;
  --bg-secondary: #f5f5f7;
  --border: #d1d1d6;
  --border-light: #e3e3e6;
  --brand-ink: #37477b;
  --brand-tint: #e4e9f7;
  --brand-line: #c9d2f2;
  --app-sheet-line: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
  --app-sheet-shadow-near: rgba(24, 39, 75, 0.12);
  --app-sheet-shadow-far: rgba(24, 39, 75, 0.08);
}
/* THE ICON TILE IS THE ONE SURFACE THE CARRIED TOKENS CANNOT REACH ON THEIR
   OWN. .app-icon-tile draws its face from --bg-primary and its ring from
   --border-light, and dark mode does not simply hand those tokens dark
   values for it: `.dark .app-icon-tile` steps the face UP to --bg-secondary
   and the ring DOWN to --border, because --bg-primary is near-black and a
   tile painted in it reads as a hole in the page rather than a surface on
   it (that rule carries the whole argument). The step is keyed to the
   CLASS, so under a tone it never happened — the app's own icon stayed a
   white square on the near-black launch cover, beside the name that had
   gone invisible. These are that rule's twins, scoped to the sheet the tone
   owns and reading the tokens carried above. Same reason the chip's
   subtitle is re-inked by hand below. */
[data-app-tone="dark"]:not(.dark) #app-frame-host .app-icon-tile {
  background-color: var(--bg-secondary);
  border-color: var(--border);
}
.dark[data-app-tone="light"] #app-frame-host .app-icon-tile {
  background-color: var(--bg-primary);
  border-color: var(--border-light);
}
[data-app-tone="dark"]:not(.dark) #platform-header #app-switcher-subtitle {
  color: #8e8e93; /* zinc-400 */
}
.dark[data-app-tone="light"] #platform-header #app-switcher-subtitle {
  color: #68686c; /* zinc-500 */
}
/* …AND THE SIDEBAR BESIDE THE APP. On a desktop the rail stays docked beside
   a running app (App._railBesideApp), so it is part of the strip around the
   app just as the bar is, and it takes the same tone: with the shell's own
   values it drew a pale column (a white half-fill over the toned, night
   wallpaper) beside a dark bar, or a night one beside a light bar.

   A DARK APP UNDER THE LIGHT SHELL puts `.dark` on the rail and its parked
   foot (lib/surface-tone.ts, the way the sheets over a dark app follow it,
   #2803), so the theme's own dark palette draws them: fill, ink, lit row,
   counts and tiles, with nothing restated here.

   A LIGHT APP UNDER THE DARK SHELL cannot do that (`.dark` cannot be taken
   away below a dark <html>), so the light values are carried here, the
   theme's own from `:root`, as the bar's are above. The two plane fills are
   set on the rail itself rather than as tokens: `--dc-sheet-fill` and
   `--dc-sheet-solid` are declared once per theme and nowhere else
   (tests/no-glass-one-scheme.test.js), so the rail paints `:root`'s values
   directly, on the desktop, where it is a rail. */
.dark[data-app-tone="light"] #platform-tabs,
.dark[data-app-tone="light"] #platform-parked {
  color: #1c1c1e; /* zinc-900, the body's light ink */
  color-scheme: light;
  --text-primary: #0a0a0a;
  --text-secondary: #3a3a3c;
  --text-muted: #68686c;
  --text-faint: #8e8e93;
  --bg-primary: #ffffff;
  --bg-secondary: #f5f5f7;
  --border: #d1d1d6;
  --border-light: #e3e3e6;
  --app-sheet-line: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
  --lit-ink: #0062cc;
  --lit-tint: #eaf4ff;
  --lit-line: rgba(10, 110, 224, 0.25);
  --accent: #0a6ee0;
  --accent-ink: #ffffff;
}
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .dark[data-app-tone="light"] #platform-tabs {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50); /* :root's --dc-sheet-fill */
  }
  .dark[data-app-tone="light"] #platform-tabs.platform-tabs-peek {
    background-color: #fffff5; /* :root's --dc-sheet-solid */
  }
}
/* Its app tiles take the light theme's face and ring, as the sheet's do
   (see "THE ICON TILE IS THE ONE SURFACE" above). */
.dark[data-app-tone="light"] :is(#platform-tabs, #platform-parked) .app-icon-tile {
  background-color: var(--bg-primary);
  border-color: var(--border-light);
}
/* AND WHAT OPENS OVER A DARK APP IS DARK TOO (#2803). This used to be the
   one thing the tone left alone: every sheet, dialog and menu kept the
   viewer's own mode, so Send feedback and the Homeroom menu opened as cream
   cards over a night-black app under a bar that had already gone dark. They
   follow the app now. lib/surface-tone.ts puts `.dark` on each kit shell
   (modal, sheet, panel, action sheet, popover, alert) and on the two in-tree
   sheets while `data-app-tone="dark"` holds under the light shell, marked
   with this class so it only removes its own. `.dark` is all the palette
   needs — the token blocks here and in native.css are plain `.dark { … }`
   and `dark:` utilities compile to `.dark .dark\:…` — so a toned surface is
   drawn exactly as it is under the dark shell.

   The one thing `.dark` on the surface cannot carry is the INK: the body's is
   `text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-100`, keyed on <html>, and these shells
   inherit it from <body>. So it is set here, the same literal the bar's tone
   uses. `:where()` keeps this at zero specificity: a kit rule that inks its
   own surface from a token still wins, and reads the dark token anyway.

   A light app under the dark shell is out of scope, as the request says:
   that direction would need `.dark` taken AWAY below a dark <html>. */
:where(.platform-app-tone-dark) {
  color: #eaeaea; /* zinc-100, the body's dark ink */
  color-scheme: dark;
}
/* THE CANVAS UNDER THE PUSH is the ground too — <html>'s own background,
   set inline in the head (frontend/src/head.html) so the first paint is
   never white, moved from the old zinc greys to the wallpaper's base in
   each mode when the wallpaper became the platform's ground. It is what
   the page transition shows wherever its snapshots do not reach: the seam
   of the iOS push and a frame of the Android fade. Nothing route-scoped
   is needed for it any more; theme-color and the manifest carry the same
   value, so the browser chrome and a cold launch frame the page in it.
   The launch cover reads the same ground, so the app opens on the page's
   colour in both modes. */
body:has(:is(#home-screen, #app-view, #leaderboard-screen, #profile-screen, #settings-screen, #messages-screen, #workshop-screen, #weekly-digest-screen, #communities-screen, #ai-screen, #profile-proposals-screen, #auth-landing-screen, #auth-waiting-screen, #auth-waitlist-screen, #auth-more-screen):not(.hidden)) .app-launch-cover {
  background-color: var(--home-ground);
}
/* THE LANDING ROOT PAINTS THE WALLPAPER ITSELF. #auth-landing-screen is a
   fixed, full-viewport overlay with its own opaque `bg-white` (it covers
   whatever the signed-in shell has left on the page), so the body's
   wallpaper never showed through it. It takes the same shorthand the body
   paints — inherited from the body's rule, which is on because this root
   is showing — in both modes. */
#auth-landing-screen,
#auth-waiting-screen,
#auth-waitlist-screen,
#auth-more-screen {
  background: var(--home-wallpaper);
}

/* ── THE WEBSITE'S LOOK ON THE SIGNED-OUT PAGES (13 Nov 2026) ────────────
   The guest Discover (its story and its list) and the page about private
   communities are a visitor's next click after onhomeroom.com, so they are
   drawn in that site's own look: a sky band with a white window in it,
   serif headlines, sun pills on an ink outline, outlined cards, and bands
   of colour down the page. The app's own look starts once somebody is in.

   The two components that are only ever drawn here
   (frontend/src/features/auth/story.tsx's short story and
   ../guest/guest-communities.tsx) spell every colour as one of the
   variables below. The pieces they share with the signed-in app (the bar's
   button, the search, the chips, the cards) keep their classes and are
   repainted from here, by their own hooks, under #auth-landing-screen only.

   The dark shell gets the same drawing on its own ground: the second rule
   moves the variables and nothing else. Sun keeps its ink words in both.
   Young Serif and Geist are the faces the new look (the last block of this
   sheet) declares; a browser downloads them only where a rule that names
   them matches, which is now the new look and these two pages
   (tests/new-look.test.js). This block sits with the landing's own rules,
   well before the looks' hand-written blocks and generated section, which
   have to stay together and directly before the new look's. */
#auth-landing-screen {
  --site-serif: 'Young Serif', Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;
  --site-display: 'Source Serif 4', 'Young Serif', Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;
  --site-sans: 'Geist', ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif, "Apple Color Emoji", "Segoe UI Emoji", "Segoe UI Symbol", "Noto Color Emoji";
  --site-ink: #121a0e;
  --site-muted: #41483c;
  --site-label: #565c53;
  --site-line: #121a0e;
  --site-sky: #2a8ce1;
  --site-ground: #fffeea;
  --site-panel: #ffffff;
  --site-sun: #ffee6f;
  --site-sun-lit: #fff4a0;
  --site-sun-pale: #fff6ac;
  --site-sky-pale: #cfe6ff;
  --site-mint: #d9ecd0;
  --site-on-sun: #121a0e;
  --site-chosen: #121a0e;
  --site-chosen-ink: #fffeea;
  /* The private page's: a hairline between rows, and its two tinted cards
     with the words on them. */
  --site-line-soft: rgba(18, 26, 14, 0.2);
  --site-card-sky: #e5f0fc;
  --site-card-leaf: #e8f3e2;
  --site-card-words: #3f4650;
  /* Words on its green and its soft sky, which are the same in both modes. */
  --site-on-band: #000000;
}
.dark #auth-landing-screen {
  /* THE SITE AT NIGHT (17 Nov 2026). The same poster with the lights off:
     the ground and the cards are the navy of the brand look's dark mode
     (--bl-page, --bl-card), the words and the outlines the site's cream, a
     dark sky with stars, and sun still sun. It used to be transparent over
     the app's dark wallpaper, whose red and purple washes are no colour of
     this site. */
  --site-ink: #fffeea;
  --site-muted: #c9ccb6;
  --site-label: #a8ad97;
  --site-line: rgba(255, 254, 234, 0.46);
  --site-sky: #0b1f43;
  --site-ground: #0d1421;
  --site-panel: #182135;
  --site-sun-pale: transparent;
  --site-sky-pale: #173560;
  --site-mint: #2f4d2b;
  --site-chosen: #fffeea;
  --site-chosen-ink: #0d1421;
  --site-line-soft: rgba(255, 254, 234, 0.18);
  --site-card-sky: #173560;
  --site-card-leaf: #2f4d2b;
  --site-card-words: #c9ccb6;
}
/* One colour edge to edge, around content that keeps the column's width:
   the band's own fill, let out to both sides as a border image (a flat
   one-colour image with an outset; it is paint only, so the scroller gains
   no width). The outset is the usable box's width, the one width this
   sheet reads (THE USABLE BOX), which always reaches both edges from inside
   the column. Not a spread shadow: this sheet keeps every shadow bounded
   (tests/kit-surfaces-frost.test.js). A component names the colour
   (`[--band:var(--site-sky)]`). */
#auth-landing-screen .site-band {
  background-color: var(--band);
  border-image: conic-gradient(var(--band) 0 0) fill 0 / 0 / 0 var(--platform-usable-w);
}
/* The sky band is the website's own sky, clouds and all, in light. It is a
   picture, so it is laid behind the band as wide as the usable box rather
   than as a border image, which would stretch it. Dark has no sky band. */
#auth-landing-screen .site-clouds { position: relative; isolation: isolate; }
#auth-landing-screen .site-clouds::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  z-index: -1;
  inset-block: 0;
  left: 50%;
  width: var(--platform-usable-w);
  transform: translateX(-50%);
  background: var(--site-sky) url('/brand/sky.webp') center / cover no-repeat;
}
/* At night the band is a dark sky with stars in place of the clouds (the
   clouds read oddly dimmed). The stars are two tiles of small dots, of
   different sizes so the pattern does not show, over a deep blue that is
   darkest at the top. */
.dark #auth-landing-screen .site-clouds::before {
  background-color: var(--site-sky);
  background-image:
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 481px 246px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 105px 102px, #ffee6f 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(2.0px 2.0px at 322px 103px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 54px 283px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(2.0px 2.0px at 29px 312px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(2.0px 2.0px at 342px 323px, #ffee6f 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 433px 278px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 458px 131px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 175px 233px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 127px 335px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 95px 242px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 572px 50px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 243px 270px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 118px 213px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 76px 16px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(2.0px 2.0px at 222px 34px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 414px 222px, #ffee6f 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 699px 146px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    radial-gradient(1.2px 1.2px at 23px 217px, #fffeea 98%, transparent),
    linear-gradient(180deg, #050d1c 0%, #0b1f43 100%);
  background-size: 520px 360px, 520px 360px, 520px 360px, 520px 360px, 520px 360px, 520px 360px, 520px 360px, 520px 360px, 520px 360px, 520px 360px, 730px 410px, 730px 410px, 730px 410px, 730px 410px, 730px 410px, 730px 410px, 730px 410px, 730px 410px, 730px 410px, auto;
  background-repeat: repeat;
}
/* The ground under the two pages is the site's cream, flat: the washes of
   the app's wallpaper are the app's. The bar's faked glass follows it. */
html:root #auth-landing-screen:has(:is([data-guest-discover], [data-guest-groups])) {
  background: var(--site-ground);
}
html:root #auth-landing-screen:has(:is([data-guest-discover], [data-guest-groups])) #landing-header {
  --fake-glass-tint: var(--site-ground);
}
/* Words on the two pages are the site's sans. */
#auth-landing-screen :is([data-guest-discover], [data-guest-groups]) {
  font-family: var(--site-sans);
}
/* The bar's button on every signed-out page is the SECONDARY pill: the
   panel's white on an ink outline, the hero's white twin (27 Nov 2026). The
   sun is each page's own main action (Explore apps, Sign up to vote and
   suggest), so a page shows one sun pill and not two. The primitive's
   classes stay (tests/shell-primitive-adoption.test.js); its fill is
   repainted. The heroes' own pills are spelled in features/auth/story.tsx. */
#auth-landing-screen #landing-header [data-landing-sign-up] {
  background-color: var(--site-panel);
  color: var(--site-ink);
  font-family: var(--site-sans);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--site-line), 0 2px 0 var(--site-line);
}
#auth-landing-screen #landing-header [data-landing-sign-up]:hover {
  background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--site-ink) 6%, var(--site-panel));
}
#auth-landing-screen #landing-header [data-landing-sign-in] {
  font-family: var(--site-sans);
}
/* THE LIST. Its heading is a title in the serif, not a label over a card;
   the search and the chips are outlined, the chosen chip is ink; a card is
   a white face on an ink outline with its name in the serif. */
#auth-landing-screen [data-guest-discover] [data-home-join-section] > .home-area-label > span:first-child {
  font-family: var(--site-serif);
  font-size: 28px;
  line-height: 34px;
  font-weight: 400;
  letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  text-transform: none;
  color: var(--site-ink);
}
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  #auth-landing-screen [data-guest-discover] [data-home-join-section] > .home-area-label > span:first-child {
    font-size: 34px;
    line-height: 40px;
  }
}
#auth-landing-screen [data-guest-discover] :is([data-guest-search-open], [data-home-join-kind], [data-home-join-sort]) {
  background-color: var(--site-panel);
  color: var(--site-ink);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--site-line);
}
/* The sort menu is one of the chips (`.wip-sort`): its chevron takes the ink too. */
#auth-landing-screen [data-guest-discover] .wip-sort { color: var(--site-ink); }
#auth-landing-screen [data-guest-discover] [data-guest-search-open] {
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--site-line), 0 2px 0 var(--site-line);
}
#auth-landing-screen [data-guest-discover] [data-home-join-kind][aria-pressed="true"] {
  background-color: var(--site-chosen);
  color: var(--site-chosen-ink);
}
#auth-landing-screen [data-guest-discover] label:has(> [data-guest-search]) {
  background-color: var(--site-panel);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--site-line);
}
/* The field has its own × (which also puts it away), so the browser's
   clear button beside it was a second one doing half the job. */
#auth-landing-screen [data-guest-discover] [data-guest-search]::-webkit-search-cancel-button {
  -webkit-appearance: none;
  display: none;
}
#auth-landing-screen [data-guest-discover] :is(#guest-join-featured-label, [data-home-join-rest]) {
  color: var(--site-label);
  font-weight: 500;
}
/* A community's card is the community card (A COMMUNITY'S CARD, approved
   20 Nov 2026) in the site's own colours: its white panel on an ink outline
   with a hard edge under it, 14px corners, its ink and muted words, and its
   name in the serif at the card's own size. The larger first row of
   Featured is the card's own larger size. */
#auth-landing-screen [data-guest-discover] {
  --cm-bg: var(--site-panel);
  --cm-radius: 14px;
  --cm-edge: 0 2px 0 var(--site-line);
  --cm-edge-on: 0 2px 0 var(--site-line);
  --cm-edge-lift: 0 2px 0 var(--site-line), 0 22px 36px -14px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.34);
  --cm-ink: var(--site-ink);
  --cm-muted: var(--site-muted);
  --cm-body: var(--site-muted);
}
#auth-landing-screen [data-guest-discover] .cm-card {
  border: 1px solid var(--site-line);
}
#auth-landing-screen [data-guest-discover] [data-guest-card-link] {
  font-family: var(--site-serif);
  font-weight: 400;
  letter-spacing: -0.005em;
}
/* ── THE PAGE ABOUT PRIVATE COMMUNITIES (the B3 design, 23 Nov 2026) ─────
   frontend/src/features/guest/guest-communities.tsx. Top to bottom: the
   hero (its words beside a walk through a community, played as four
   scenes), what a group gets on the green with stars, who it is for, the
   approval rules on the green again, and a closing band on a soft sky.

   Its headings are the hero's serif at every size (--site-serif is Source
   Serif 4 here; the landing's list keeps Young Serif).

   THE WALK-THROUGH IS A PICTURE OF THE APP, so it keeps one palette of its
   own in both modes, as a screenshot would: its window is white on a dark
   page too. Its classes (hr-, hx-, sc-) are the design file's. */
#auth-landing-screen [data-guest-groups] {
  --site-serif: 'Source Serif 4', Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;
}
/* A picture edge to edge behind a band, laid as the clouds are. */
#auth-landing-screen :is(.site-stars, .site-sky-soft) { position: relative; isolation: isolate; }
#auth-landing-screen :is(.site-stars, .site-sky-soft)::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  z-index: -1;
  inset-block: 0;
  left: 50%;
  width: var(--platform-usable-w);
  transform: translateX(-50%);
}
#auth-landing-screen .site-stars::before { background: #a9cf97 url('/brand/groups/stars.webp') center / cover no-repeat; }
#auth-landing-screen .site-sky-soft::before { background: #8ec3e6 url('/brand/bg/sky.webp') center / cover no-repeat; }

/* The hero's three points, and the column the walk-through sits in. */
#auth-landing-screen .hr-points { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center; gap: 6px 18px; margin-top: 22px; font-family: var(--site-sans); font-size: 14px; line-height: 20px; color: var(--site-muted); }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-points li { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 7px; }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-points svg { width: 16px; height: 16px; flex-shrink: 0; color: var(--site-ink); }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo-col { min-width: 0; padding: 4px 12px 20px; }
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  #auth-landing-screen .hr-demo-col { padding: 8px 28px 32px; }
}
@media (min-width: 1024px) {
  #auth-landing-screen .hr-points { flex-direction: column; justify-content: flex-start; }
  #auth-landing-screen .hr-demo-col { display: flex; align-items: center; padding: 36px 32px; background: var(--site-ground); border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--site-line); }
}

/* THE WALK-THROUGH: an airy window of the app (soft white cards, blue and
   sun touches) over the sky. */
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo {
  --ink: #14161a; --mute: #5e6470; --faint: #9aa0aa; --line: rgba(20, 30, 50, 0.08); --wash: #f5f8fc;
  --blue: #2f8fe3; --blue-ink: #1f6fc4; --blue-pale: #e5f0fc; --sun: #ffee6f; --sun-pale: #fff8d6; --green-pale: #e8f3e2;
  --card-shadow: 0 0 0 1px rgba(20, 30, 50, 0.05), 0 1px 2px rgba(20, 30, 50, 0.05), 0 6px 18px -8px rgba(20, 30, 50, 0.12);
  width: 100%; max-width: 600px; margin: 0 auto; font-family: var(--site-sans); color: var(--ink); text-align: start; letter-spacing: -0.01em;
}
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-win { position: relative; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 16px; background: #fff; box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px rgba(20, 30, 50, 0.1), 0 2px 4px rgba(20, 30, 50, 0.05), 0 34px 70px -34px rgba(20, 40, 80, 0.45); }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-body { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 156px minmax(0, 1fr); height: 488px; }

/* Its sidebar. */
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-side { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1px; padding: 14px 8px 10px; background: #fff; border-inline-end: 1px solid var(--line); color: var(--ink); font-size: 12px; font-weight: 500; overflow: hidden; }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-logo { height: 17px; width: auto; margin: 0 8px 12px; color: var(--ink); }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-nav { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; height: 28px; padding: 0 8px; border-radius: 8px; white-space: nowrap; transition: background 250ms, color 250ms; }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-nav svg { width: 14px; height: 14px; flex-shrink: 0; color: #3b414b; }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-nav em { margin-inline-start: auto; min-width: 16px; height: 16px; padding: 0 4px; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--sun); color: var(--ink); font-size: 10px; font-weight: 600; font-style: normal; line-height: 16px; text-align: center; }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-nav.is-on { background: var(--blue-pale); color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-nav.is-on svg { color: var(--blue-ink); }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-side small { margin: 10px 8px 4px; font-size: 10.5px; font-weight: 500; color: var(--faint); }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-sep { height: 1px; margin: 6px 8px; background: var(--line); }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-comm { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 7px; height: 28px; padding: 0 8px; font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap; min-width: 0; }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-comm span:last-child { overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-tree { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1px; margin-inline-start: 17px; padding-inline-start: 5px; border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--line); }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-tile { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; width: 20px; height: 20px; flex-shrink: 0; border-radius: 6px; background: var(--sun-pale); box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(20, 30, 50, 0.06); color: var(--ink); }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-tile svg { width: 13px; height: 13px; }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-tile.lg { width: 34px; height: 34px; border-radius: 10px; }
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-tile.lg svg { width: 20px; height: 20px; }

/* Its main area: a soft sky at the top, fading to white. */
#auth-landing-screen .hr-demo .hx-main {
  position: relative; min-width: 0; overflow: hidden;
