/* ── THE LANDING'S ACTIVITY RAIL LOOPS ─────────────────────────────────
   The signed-out landing draws four chips wider than a phone
   (frontend/src/features/auth/landing.tsx). The overflow is the point — it
   says more goes on here than fits — but a strip that only overflows reads
   as a row somebody forgot to finish. Looping it says the same thing on
   purpose.

   The track holds the four chips TWICE and travels exactly half its own
   width, so the second copy arrives where the first began and the seam
   never shows. Half is exact only because each chip carries its 8px as a
   right MARGIN rather than the row carrying `gap`: with `gap` the track is
   eight chips plus seven gaps, and half of that lands mid-gap and stutters
   once per cycle. The second copy is aria-hidden in the markup, so a reader
   hears the four lines once.

   34s is a deliberate amble — about 25px a second. Fast enough to be
   clearly alive on a screen somebody looks at for a few seconds, slow
   enough not to pull the eye off the heading, which is the thing this
   screen is actually for.

   The transform runs on the compositor (translate3d, will-change) because
   this is the one thing animating on a screen that also decodes a 90KB
   illustration on first paint. */
.landing-rail-track {
  display: flex;
  width: max-content;
  will-change: transform;
  animation: landing-rail-loop 34s linear infinite;
}
@keyframes landing-rail-loop {
  from { transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0); }
  to { transform: translate3d(-50%, 0, 0); }
}
/* Decoration, so it STOPS rather than slows: a reader who asked for less
   motion still gets the whole row, still overflowing, simply still. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .landing-rail-track { animation: none; }
}
/* Reduce motion setting (#6510): the same, when Settings asks for it. */
:where(html[data-motion="reduce"]) .landing-rail-track { animation: none; }

/* ── App cards ─────────────────────────────────────────── */

.app-card { transition: background-color 150ms; }
/* A pointer that can actually hover only (#2892). On a touch screen WebKit
   emulates :hover from the last tap and keeps it until the next tap lands
   somewhere else — so a tile held for its menu kept this fill as a stuck
   "selected" look, most visibly after "Add to Homeroom widget", the one menu
   action that leaves you on the home screen with the tile still in view. */
@media (hover: hover) {
  .app-card:hover { background-color: var(--bg-card); }
}

/* The widget strip as a DROP TARGET (#2894): a "Your apps" tile dragged over
   it. home.js (_markWidgetDrop) sets `data-drop` for the span of the hover —
   an attribute because the strip's className is React's. `ok` is the same
   tint as the grid's own target cells (.home-grid-cell--on); `refused` (already pinned, or the widget is
   full) is a muted outline that says "not here" without shouting. */
#widget-strip[data-drop="ok"] {
  border-style: solid;
  border-color: rgba(31, 134, 255, 0.75);
  background-color: rgba(31, 134, 255, 0.14);
}
#widget-strip[data-drop="refused"] {
  border-color: rgb(161 161 170);
  opacity: 0.7;
}

/* THE TILE LABEL — iOS-homescreen sized (#951).
   11px on a 13px line, TWO lines, ellipsised on overflow. #4934 tried one
   step up (12px on a 14px line) because over the wallpaper's washes the
   label read tiny — but on a phone's four columns the bigger type cut most
   names after a few letters (#5195), so it is back here. One pixel of type
   and one of line height is what a step up would cost: the two-line lane
   would be 2px taller, and the --home-cell-h derivation below would grow
   by the same 2px. The colour is inherited from the
   body's text-zinc-900 / dark:text-zinc-100, which clears 13:1 on the
   cream ground and its washes and 10:1 on the darkest dark wash (the
   ember); the label sets no colour of its own so the Create tile's muted
   ink still applies.

   Before #951 the label was one 14px/20px line with `truncate`, which cut
   most real app names after ~12 characters; at this size and two lines a tile shows roughly
   three times as much of a long name.

   The lane measures exactly two lines (26px, 2 x 13) as a MIN-height, not
   a fixed height: a one-line title and a two-line title still occupy the
   same box at the design size, so the caption below a tile — and the
   tile's whole content height — does not shift per tile, and the 124px
   budget in the --home-cell-h derivation is unchanged. But the box now
   GROWS when a device renders the font larger than the design size (#3661):
   Android's text scaling / font boosting inflates this label's font-size
   and line-height (~14px on ~17px lines) while rem-based lengths stay put,
   and the old fixed-height lane clipped that second line mid-glyph. With
   the floor the inflated text simply takes its two lines' worth of room —
   at most a few pixels into the row gap on such a device, never a cut-off
   word. The grid's rows themselves stay fixed; only the label's own box
   is content-sized beyond its floor.

   line-height is unitless (13px at the design size, 13/11) for the same
   reason: it tracks whatever font-size the device actually renders, so
   the two line boxes scale with the glyphs instead of staying 13px under
   them.

   -webkit-line-clamp is the only cross-browser way to ellipsise at a
   line COUNT (the standard `line-clamp` is listed alongside it for the
   engines that have shipped it unprefixed); it needs the -webkit-box
   display, which is why this is a class rather than Tailwind's
   `truncate`. overflow-wrap: anywhere keeps a single long unbroken word
   (a slug-style name) inside the tile instead of overflowing it.

   HYPHENATED FIRST (overnight audit L05, 2 Nov 2026). On a 13 mini a tile's
   text lane is about 60px, narrower than "Neighbourhood" at 11px, so
   `anywhere` alone cut it at the last letter that fit ("Neighbourh / ood
   Swap"). `hyphens: auto` breaks such a word at a syllable with a hyphen,
   in the document's language (<html lang>, set by the i18n runtime);
   `anywhere` stays as the last resort for a word the dictionary cannot
   split. */
.app-card-title {
  display: -webkit-box;
  -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
  -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
  line-clamp: 2;
  overflow: hidden;
  width: 100%;
  min-height: 1.625rem; /* 26px — exactly two lines at the design size */
  font-size: 0.6875rem; /* 11px */
  line-height: 1.18; /* 13px at the design size; unitless so it tracks the font */
  font-weight: 500;
  text-align: center;
  /* A WORD BREAKS ONLY WHEN IT CANNOT FIT A LINE ON ITS OWN (phone review,
     23 Nov 2026). `hyphens: auto` with `anywhere` let WebKit fill the first
     line with a piece of the second word: "Pourover Cof-fee", "Game Cor-ner",
     beside a neighbour that wrapped at its space. A name's own soft hyphens
     still count (manual), and a single word wider than the tile is still
     cut to fit rather than run out of it. */
  overflow-wrap: break-word;
  word-break: normal;
  -webkit-hyphens: manual;
  hyphens: manual;
}

/* The status caption ("Spinning up…" / "Awaiting secrets" / "Error")
   steps down with it: 11px on a 12px line, no top margin, so the two
   text lanes together are 26 + 12 = 38px — the same as the old
   20 (title) + 16 (caption) + 2 (mt-0.5) budget. */
.app-card-status {
  font-size: 0.6875rem; /* 11px */
  line-height: 0.75rem; /* 12px */
}
/* "Opened 2h ago" (#4025): when you last left the app, in the caption lane
   a running app leaves empty, so the row's fixed height does not move. One
   line, muted, and never cut: a line that is only a time says nothing with
   its end missing. Under 640px, where the grid's four columns leave a text
   lane of 69px on a 375px screen (55px at 320px), the sentence (about 91px
   at its longest, "Opened 59m ago") gives way to the time alone, 46px at
   its longest. The tile's aria-label keeps the sentence. */
.app-card-opened {
  white-space: nowrap;
  color: var(--text-muted);
}
.app-card-opened-short { display: none; }
@media (max-width: 639.98px) {
  .app-card-opened-long { display: none; }
  .app-card-opened-short { display: inline; }
}

/* ── App icon tiles ────────────────────────────────────────
   The single treatment for every homescreen-style app icon tile:
   home cards, the widget strip and the landing (unauthed) grid. A plain
   white face (an elevated
   zinc surface in dark mode) with a faint hairline tracing whatever
   rounded shape the call site picks (rounded-xl on the 56px tiles,
   rounded-lg on the 40px widget tiles) — the border is drawn by this
   class, the radius by the element's own Tailwind class, so the ring
   always follows the tile.

   The hairline uses a DIFFERENT token per mode. --border-light is
   inverted between the two palettes: in light mode it is fainter than
   --border (#e4e4e7 vs #d4d4d8), but in dark mode it is stronger
   (#484870 vs #2e2e50). Using it in both would make the tile ring the
   most contrasty border on a dark page, wrapped around every icon —
   so dark mode steps down to --border instead (see below). The token
   itself is left alone; 17 other rules depend on its current value.

   The glyph inherits `color` from here. Image icons still fill the
   tile: they're `w-full h-full object-cover` inside the border box, so
   the image sits flush inside the hairline.

   Tile call sites must NOT set their own background / text colour
   utilities — routing every tile through this one class is the point. */
.app-icon-tile {
  background-color: var(--bg-primary);
  border: 1px solid var(--border-light);
  color: var(--text-secondary);
}
/* THE PER-APP IDENTITY TINT IS GONE. The reskin hashed each app's slug into
   one of six pastels and painted the tile with it (`--tile-lime` and friends,
   `.app-icon-tile[data-tint="…"]`). A launcher of six unrelated pastels reads
   as six unrelated things rather than as one shelf, and an app's icon is its
   own artwork — the tile should hold it, not compete with it. One neutral
   face everywhere, which is what the rule above already draws.

   Nothing sets `data-tint` any more, so the `:not([data-tint])` guards that
   protected the two rules below from it are gone with it: the specificity
   trap they described — both selectors at (0,2,0), source order deciding —
   cannot recur without the attribute. */
/* The first-letter fallback specifically — one step fainter than the base
   glyph colour so a name initial reads as a quiet placeholder rather than as
   content. Tiles tag their kind with data-icon, so this never touches emoji
   tiles (whose colour glyphs ignore `color` anyway) or image tiles. Both modes
   step to the same faint token. */
.app-icon-tile[data-icon="letter"] {
  color: var(--text-faint);
}
/* An image tile's initial while its picture loads (AppIconContent's
   IconImage, polish board #11): the letter tile's own quiet placeholder,
   laid under the image, and gone once the image has drawn. The tile is its
   box at zero specificity, so a tile that already positions itself keeps
   doing so. */
:where(:has(> .app-icon-wait)) { position: relative; }
.app-icon-wait {
  position: absolute; inset: 0;
  display: grid; place-items: center;
  color: var(--text-faint); font-weight: 700; line-height: 1;
  pointer-events: none;
}
.dark .app-icon-tile {
  /* --bg-primary is near-black in dark mode; one step up so the tile
     reads as a surface sitting on the page rather than a hole in it. */
  background-color: var(--bg-secondary);
  /* …and back DOWN one step for the ring: --border-light is the
     brighter of the two hairline tokens in dark mode (see above), so
     the faint-hairline intent needs --border here. */
  border-color: var(--border);
}
/* Phone review #92 (23 Nov 2026): a ring over every app's icon in dark, its
   picture included. A black icon (Homeroom's own) filled its tile edge to
   edge, so on the near-black thread and card grounds the tile went and only
   its white mark was left, smaller than and out of line with people's discs.
   An outline is drawn over the picture, where the tile's border is under it,
   and it follows the corners. Homeroom bot's face is the same picture drawn
   outside a tile (a thread's head, its messages, Your work), so it is ringed
   here too; the header's own mark keeps its look. */
.dark :is(.app-icon-tile, img[src$="/brand/homeroom-mark.png"]:not(.platform-mark-tile)) {
  outline: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14);
  outline-offset: -1px;
}
/* Drag-reorderable starred cards (issue #191): a long-press must
   pick the card up, not select its title text or pop the mobile
   copy/look-up callout. Static rather than toggled at drag start
   because selection begins during the ~350ms long-press window,
   before any JS drag state exists. Scoped to the drag-enabled
   class (added in home.js only when reordering is possible) so
   text everywhere else on the page selects normally. */
.app-card-draggable {
  user-select: none;
  -webkit-user-select: none;
  -webkit-touch-callout: none;
  -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
}

/* ── Home section headers ──────────────────────────────── */

.home-section-header {
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  color: var(--text-muted);
  padding: 0.25rem 0;
}

/* The home screen's content column: everything in the feed — the search
   bar, the "Your apps" grid, and the trailing Featured / Create sections —
   is capped at 1024px and centred in the viewport, so a wide desktop
   window reads as one column instead of a grid stretched edge to edge.

   The cap INCLUDES each block's own horizontal gutter (the px-3 / p-3 the
   children carry), which is why the search bar's padding lives on its
   inner .home-column rather than on the full-bleed bar: both columns then
   have identical content edges. Below 1024px this is a no-op and the
   gutters are the only horizontal padding, which is what a phone wants.

   Inside the column the trailing sections' boxes are FULL WIDTH — they
   used to be narrower and indented to line up with the leftmost app icon,
   a measured offset that existed only because the column itself was
   unbounded. Centring the column is the better answer, so don't
   re-introduce a per-box width bound. */
.home-column {
  width: 100%;
  max-width: 64rem; /* 1024px */
  margin-inline-start: auto;
  margin-inline-end: auto;
}

/* ── Home-screen sections (#911) ────────────────────────────
   Discover, Challenges and Create app: three bordered blocks stacked under
   the launcher grid, in that order, each a plain full-width child of its own
   <section>. THE UI OVERHAUL fixed them there. They were draggable ITEMS of
   #app-list before it — a `.home-panel-slot` host at a stored (column, row)
   cell, spanning real cells so app icons flowed around them the iOS-widget
   way — and everything below that answered to a rectangle: a two-cell height
   cap, a half-column width cap, a stretch-to-fill, and a phone branch that
   reshaped the content to fit ONE 116px cell. All of it is gone with the
   placement. A section is as tall as it draws and as wide as .home-column.

   HEIGHT — nothing survives the rectangle. The block used to carry
   --home-panel-max-h ("about two rows of app icons", 16rem, derived from
   --home-cell-h), enforced by flex — flex-none title bar and footer, a rows
   list with min-height: 0 and overflow: hidden — so content CLIPPED at the
   ceiling instead of growing the tile, with .home-panel--expanded lifting it.

   A section grows. What bounds the Challenges block is the MARKUP:
   HomePanels.challengesView draws a short pick (every First challenge, or
   at most three Next ones) and "See all" in its heading goes to the
   Challenges tab.

   THE LEADERBOARD FILL (.home-panel-fill) was a standings preview under the
   challenge rows, and it is REMOVED — two labelled lists with two different
   tap destinations inside one card called Challenges. The standings are a
   screen, and the section's heading carries the one tap to it. Its rules are
   gone from this file, and so is the heading's own "Open challenges" link
   (`.home-panel-lb-browse`), replaced by "See all" (`.home-panel-see-all`).

   NO WIDTH CAP any more. It was --home-panel-max-w, half of .home-column
   (512px of 1024px), and the argument for it was "a widget is not a section":
   a challenges row is one short line plus two small chips, and stretching it
   to 1024px left a lonely reward chip pinned to the far right. These ARE
   sections now — they are the screen's areas, stacked, each one the full
   width of the column above it — and a half-width Discover under a
   full-width app grid reads as a rendering fault, not as restraint. The
   lanes and rows inside them lay out against the width they are given.

   tests/home-panels-render.test.js pins the row budget and the tile tokens
   it was derived from, so a drift fails rather than silently mis-sizing the
   block. */
:root {
  /* ONE APP-GRID ROW. Every other height on the home screen is derived from
     it: the grid's own grid-auto-rows and the drag overlay's cells, which
     must line up with real tiles to the pixel.

     Desktop (>= 640px), from .app-card's own tokens in home.js renderAppCard:
       p-3        0.75rem x 2  = 1.5rem
       w-14 h-14  3.5rem       = 3.5rem
       gap-1.5    0.375rem     = 0.375rem
       title      2 x 13px     = 1.625rem  <- .app-card-title, two lines
       caption    12px line    = 0.75rem   <- .app-card-status, see below
                                --------
                                7.75rem   (124px)

     #951 shrank the label from one 14px/20px line to two 11px/13px lines
     and tightened the icon->label gap and the caption line to match, so
     the total is UNCHANGED and nothing derived from --home-cell-h moved.
     Both text lanes are fixed heights, so a one-line and a two-line title
     produce identical tiles.
     #4934 grew the label to 12px on 14px lines; #5195 brought it back,
     because on a phone's four columns the bigger type cut most names
     after a few letters.
     The phone variant is 116px: index.html tightens the tile to `p-2`
     (0.5rem x 2), which is 8px less. Four 56px icons per row only read as a
     home screen at that density.

     THE CAPTION LANE IS NOT OPTIONAL. Most tiles are running apps and draw
     nothing there, so it looks like dead space — but the status dot is gone
     from the tile face, which makes "Spinning up…" / "Awaiting secrets" /
     "Error" the ONLY status signal a tile has. Rows are a FIXED height (the
     8-row canvas and the drag overlay both depend on it), so a caption with
     no room doesn't push the row taller — it paints over the tile below.
     Budgeting for it is what keeps an errored app legible instead of
     bleeding into its neighbour. */
  --home-cell-h: 7.75rem; /* 124px — see the derivation above */
  /* ONE EMPTY ROW on a phone (#975). Half a cell — 58px against the phone's
     116px — so a row the viewer left empty keeps its place in the arrangement
     without reserving a whole tile to be empty. DERIVED from the cell rather
     than written out, so the two can never drift.

     Emitted by Home.rowTemplate for the rows HomeLayout.blankRows names, and
     only at four columns; the :root value here (62px) is never used, since a
     desktop grid gets no inline template at all. A variable and not an inline
     calc() because every track in that template has to stay a single
     space-free token — see rowTemplate. */
  --home-blank-row-h: calc(var(--home-cell-h) / 2);
  /* A CHALLENGE ROW IS TWO LINES NOW (56px, was 40).

     The METER LANE is gone with the third line of tokens that used to sit
     here (--home-panel-meter-lane / -bar-h / -bar-gap). That lane was a 14px
     strip reserved along the bottom of every row in a panel that had at
     least one numeric challenge, with the row's text padded clear of it — so
     the bar had somewhere to ride and every goal still sat on one baseline.

     It worked, and it cost two things nobody chose. Reserving the lane on
     EVERY row put all the text 7px above the row's true centre; and on a row
     with no bar to draw, the reserved strip was simply a hole, so a mixed
     list read as some rows aligned and some rows not.

     Both go away by giving the row a second LINE instead of a reserved strip:
     the goal, its count and its reward take line one, the track takes line
     two and runs the full width of the text column, and every challenge draws
     one — a yes-or-no challenge gets a two-state track (empty or full) rather
     than nothing. There is no shared baseline to protect because there is no
     row that lacks a bar, and the flex centring is over the whole row again.

     The track's own geometry is Tailwind utilities on the element (see
     panels/challenges.tsx) rather than derived rules here: it is an ordinary
     block in the row's second line now, so there is nothing left to derive. */
  --home-panel-row-h: 3.5rem; /* 56px — see the budget above */
}

/* NO HEIGHT CAP. `max-height: var(--home-panel-max-h)` lived here, and the
   whole apparatus below it — .home-panel-rows' overflow: hidden, the
   flex-none title bar and footer, .home-panel--expanded lifting the cap —
   existed to make a block CLIP inside the two-app-row rectangle it occupied
   on the launcher canvas.

   A section has no rectangle. What bounds the Challenges block now is the
   MARKUP: HomePanels.challengesView draws a short pick and "See all" goes to
   the Challenges tab, so there is nothing left for a second, invisible
   mechanism to bound. Keeping the cap after the
   fill stopped being leftover-sized would have clipped the standings preview
   the Challenges area now exists to show. */
.home-panel {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}

/* THE TITLE BAR IS GONE (and it WAS the drag handle, #911). It carried
   `cursor: grab` and a "Drag to move this widget" tooltip while these blocks
   were grid items; THE UI OVERHAUL fixed them into sections and took the grip
   with it, leaving a `user-select: none` strip that held a title. The title is
   the section's own label above the card now and the block's controls went
   with it (frontend/src/features/home/panels/ui.tsx), so there is no bar left
   to style — a card opens on its content.

   The label row it became is `.home-area-label`, and it needs no rule here:
   it is utilities all the way down, and DELIBERATELY not `.home-section-header`
   above, which is the 12px caption the widget strip and the search-results
   heading use. */
.home-area-label button {
  cursor: pointer;
}

/* The rows list must be allowed to SHRINK below its content height —
   min-height: 0 is what makes overflow: hidden clip instead of the flex
   item refusing to compress and pushing the article past the cap. */
.home-panel-rows {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-height: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
}

/* The challenges widget's BODY (#947): the rows list plus, on the desktop
   tile, the LEADERBOARD fill under it. It is the flex child that takes the
   article's remaining height and clips — the job .home-panel-rows used to do
   alone, moved out one level so the two stacked blocks inside it keep their
   natural heights.

   That is the whole reason this wrapper exists: with `flex: 1 1 auto` still
   on the rows list, the surplus would be absorbed BETWEEN the challenge rows
   and the leaderboard label, splitting the tile with a gap down its middle.
   Pinning both children to their content height collects the leftover at the
   BOTTOM of the tile, where it reads as ordinary padding.

   Scoped to the challenges panel; discover and create keep their markup, and
   the existing descendant selectors (`.home-panel .home-panel-rows--metered
   .home-panel-row`, asserted in dapp.json) still match through it. */
.home-panel-body {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-height: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
}
.home-panel-body > .home-panel-rows {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
}

/* `.home-panel-footer::before`, the rule above the footer, is gone. The
   Challenges block was the only one with a footer, and it sits on the page
   ground now with no divider anywhere in it (the ITERATION 03 board's Home
   screen): the 14px step between items is what separates them. */

/* Uniform height is what makes the 4-slot budget above exact. Each row is
   TWO LINES — goal/count/reward, then the track — and both are fixed, so a
   row with a long goal and a row with a short one occupy the same box.

   STRICTLY ONE LINE PER LINE. `nowrap` here is the belt to the per-element
   `whitespace-nowrap` braces in panels/challenges.tsx: a fixed-height row
   HIDES a wrap (the extra line just overflows the box and gets clipped by
   the panel), so relying on the goal's `truncate` alone and on flex to keep
   the chips honest is how a three-line row ships unnoticed. Declaring it on
   the row means every present and future child inherits it, and
   `overflow: hidden` clips rather than letting a chip spill over the
   neighbouring row.

   The whole row is the tap target into the Challenges screen and it spans
   the column, so its hit AREA is far past what the 44px minimum-dimension
   guideline protects — that rule is about small icon controls, and the one
   of those here (the ⋮ menu) keeps .un-touch-target. */
.home-panel-row {
  position: relative;
  height: var(--home-panel-row-h);
  white-space: nowrap;
  overflow: hidden;
}
/* A hairline between two stacked rows, FLUSH with the column. No markup draws
   two today: the only `.home-panel-row` is the Challenges block's
   between-seasons line, and it is alone, so this rule does not fire. It used
   to start 0.625rem in, matching that line's `px-2.5` inside the retired
   plate's rounded corners; the block is a flat column on the page ground now
   and its text starts at the heading's edge, so a rule starts there too.

   A pseudo-element rather than a border because the row is a fixed-height box
   whose height budget (--home-panel-row-h) is spent exactly: a 1px border is
   part of the border box and would make every row after the first a pixel
   shorter than the first, which is the whole reason the 4-slot budget above
   is stated in whole rows. */
.home-panel-row + .home-panel-row::before {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  inset-inline-start: 0;
  inset-inline-end: 0;
  height: 1px;
  background: var(--border-light);
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* `.home-panel-rows--metered` and the `.home-panel-bar-track` geometry rule
   are gone with the lane, and the track and fill names went with the count
   capsule that inherited them: a Home challenge card draws the Challenges
   tab's shared progress rail now (features/leaderboard/challenge-card.tsx),
   and the declared checks select on its `[role=progressbar]`. */

/* The season progress's own hairline (`.home-panel-season::after`) is gone
   with the plate it divided. `.home-panel-season` stays in the markup as the
   hook the declared checks select on, and needs no rule: the block is one flat
   column now and the progress is just its first band. */

/* The footer bar (`.home-panel-footer`, the "See all N challenges" / "Show
   less" toggle) and `.home-panel--expanded .home-panel-rows`, which stopped
   the rows list clipping while the block was expanded in place, are gone
   with the toggle: the block shows a short pick and "See all" in its heading
   goes to the Challenges tab (the Semi design, 2026-10-16). */

/* `.home-panel-slot` — the in-grid drag host — is gone with the placement.
   It spanned real cells so app icons flowed around a widget the iOS way, and
   it carried a width cap, a stretch-to-fill and a phone release from that
   stretch. THE UI OVERHAUL made the three blocks fixed <section>s, so there
   is no rectangle to fill and nothing to drag. */

/* ── The launcher grid ──────────────────────────────────────
   Free-form placement: every child is positioned at an explicit
   (column, row) by an inline `grid-column` / `grid-row` from
   Home.renderGridItem. Rows are a FIXED height so eight of them are a
   deterministic canvas and so the drag overlay's cells line up with real
   tiles to the pixel — `auto` rows would size to content and the overlay
   would drift the moment one tile had a status line and another didn't.

   position: relative anchors that overlay (below).

   The SEARCH view is exempt: it is a flat, transient list, and its "N
   results" header and empty-state line would each claim a whole tile row
   under a fixed height. Home.render() stamps data-view on the grid.

   ON A PHONE THIS IS THE DEFAULT ROW HEIGHT, NOT EVERY ROW'S (#968, #975).
   Home.render() writes an inline `grid-template-rows` below 640px — one
   entry per occupied row, `var(--home-cell-h)` for most of them plus two
   exceptions:

     * a content-sized `minmax(floor, auto)` for a row a FIT widget owns
       outright (HomeLayout.fitRows; challenges only today). Without it the
       between-seasons widget drew a ~68px box inside a 116px reservation and
       handed the other ~48px to nobody: a grid item cannot shrink a fixed
       track, so shrinking the BOX was never going to shrink the PAGE.
     * `var(--home-blank-row-h)` — half a cell — for a row with NOTHING in it
       (HomeLayout.blankRows). Same reasoning one step further: an empty row
       was spending a whole tile's height on nothing, and on the default phone
       arrangement there are two of them between the app tiles and the lower
       widgets.

   Note what shrinking a blank row is NOT: nothing re-packs, nothing collapses,
   nothing is tidied away. The row keeps its index, keeps its place in the
   arrangement and stays a drop target — a hole is still an arrangement the
   viewer chose. Only its pixel height changes, and only on a phone.

   Two things the rule deliberately does not do: it never shrinks a row
   holding an app tile (a card with no status caption is ~100px, and that lane
   is load-bearing — see --home-cell-h), which is also why a PARTLY empty row
   stays full; and it never emits more entries than there are occupied rows (an
   explicit grid exists whether or not anything is in it, so eight entries
   would pad a three-app home screen out to ~950px — which is likewise why the
   trailing empty rows below the last tile are not in the template and have
   nothing to shrink). Desktop gets no template at all and this token is still
   the whole story there. */
#app-list {
  position: relative;
  grid-auto-rows: var(--home-cell-h);
}
/* HOLD THE LAUNCHER'S SPACE UNTIL THE GRID HAS RENDERED ONCE.
   The document the service worker serves is the PRERENDER, and the prerender's
   #app-list is empty — so a cached load paints the header, five area labels
   and the account row stacked at the top of the page, and everything below the
   grid then slides down by two rows the moment the bundle hydrates and the
   skeleton appears. That is the largest single jump between the cached paint
   and the loaded one, and it is pure CSS to remove: reserve the two-row
   default (HomeLayout.DEFAULT_ROWS) that the grid is about to draw anyway.
   Keyed on `data-view`, which app-grid.tsx sets on its first READY render and
   the prerender therefore lacks — so this applies for exactly the parse-to-
   hydrate window and never to a loaded grid, including the empty one a brand
   new account has (which would otherwise be padded with two blank rows). */
#app-list:not([data-view]) {
  /* Two cells PLUS the gap between them and the grid's own padding
     (app-grid.tsx: `gap-1.5 p-2 pt-1.5`, from 640px `gap-2 p-3 pt-2`). The
     box is border-box, so two bare cells came up 20px short of the two rows
     they stand for (28px from 640px), and the sections under the grid moved
     by that much when it drew (#10). */
  min-height: calc(2 * var(--home-cell-h) + 1.25rem);
}
@media (min-width: 640px) {
  #app-list:not([data-view]) {
    min-height: calc(2 * var(--home-cell-h) + 1.75rem);
  }
}
/* …AND HOLD EVERY AREA AT THE HEIGHT THIS DEVICE LAST DREW IT (#10, 19 Oct
   2026). Two rows was the right guess for the grid alone, but the real
   launcher draws as many rows as the screen has room for, plus "Show all N
   apps", and Discover and Challenges below it land at heights no skeleton can
   know: the desktop measured one shift of 0.0585 on every cold load (Discover
   54 → 121px, Challenges pushed from y 392 to 523). The head's inline script
   puts the heights this device last drew on <html> (`first-paint-reserved`
   and the `--reserve-*` properties; frontend/src/lib/first-paint-reserve.ts
   keeps them), and each area holds its height only while it is still a
   skeleton: the grid until its first ready render (`data-view`, as above), a
   section while `.home-section-pending` (features/home/panels/sections.tsx).
   The real page then lands in boxes of its own size. `overflow: hidden` clips
   a skeleton taller than its box; nothing real is ever inside one. The
   sidebar's community rows have the same hold, beside their own rules. */
html.first-paint-reserved #home-apps-section:has(#app-list:not([data-view])) {
  height: var(--reserve-home-apps);
  overflow: hidden;
}
html.first-paint-reserved #home-discover-section.home-section-pending {
  height: var(--reserve-home-discover);
  overflow: hidden;
}
html.first-paint-reserved #home-challenges-section.home-section-pending {
  height: var(--reserve-home-challenges);
  overflow: hidden;
}
#app-list[data-view="search"] {
  grid-auto-rows: auto;
}

/* ── The drag-time grid overlay ─────────────────────────────
   While something is lifted, the grid it snaps to is drawn underneath it —
   every cell of the canvas outlined, including the empty ones, so "you can
   drop this anywhere" is visible rather than something to discover.

   It is a real GRID sharing #app-list's own gap and row height (the column
   template is set inline, since the column count is a JS-side decision), and
   it is inset by the grid's own padding. That is what makes it align with
   zero arithmetic: it is laid out by the same engine, with the same numbers,
   as the tiles it sits behind.

   The row height below is the DEFAULT and the padding for rows past the
   content (#968, #975). Since a phone row can now be shorter than a cell —
   content-sized for a fit widget, half a cell for an empty row —
   Home._showGridOverlay copies #app-list's USED tracks
   (getComputedStyle → "116px 67.5px 58px …") onto this element inline and pads
   them out to the eight-row canvas with this token. The padded rows stay a
   FULL cell on purpose: they are past the template, so they are the drag-only
   region below the content, where there is no page height to reclaim and a big
   drop target is worth more. Copied rather than
   re-derived on purpose: one element decides the row heights and the other
   reads its answer, so the two grids cannot disagree. In pixels, too — the
   cells here are empty divs, so a content-sized track would collapse to
   nothing.

   The inset must track that padding EXACTLY, including the tighter top —
   #app-list is `p-2 pt-1.5 sm:p-3 sm:pt-2`, so a uniform inset sits the whole
   overlay 2px (phone) / 4px (desktop) low. That is not just a cosmetic drift
   in the tint: Home._rectForCell measures these cells to find where a
   committed drop lands, so any offset here becomes a jump at the end of the
   release glide, exactly where the motion is supposed to be seamless.

   It doubles as the drag's HIT-TEST surface (Home._cellFromPoint), which is
   why the layer disables pointer events but the cells re-enable them: a hit
   must land on a cell, not on a tile behind it. The kit's ghost is
   pointer-events:none, so it never occludes them either. */
.home-grid-overlay {
  position: absolute;
  /* #app-list's p-2 pt-1.5 on a phone */
  inset: 0.375rem 0.5rem 0.5rem;
  display: grid;
  gap: 0.375rem;            /* gap-1.5, matching the phone grid */
  grid-auto-rows: var(--home-cell-h);
  pointer-events: none;
  z-index: 0;
}
.home-grid-cell {
  position: relative;
  pointer-events: auto;
  border: 1px dashed var(--border-light);
  border-radius: 0.75rem;   /* rounded-xl, same as a tile */
  transition: background-color 120ms, border-color 120ms;
}
/* The cell's HIT AREA bleeds into the surrounding gap. The overlay is a real
   grid, so the gap between cells belongs to no cell — and a pointer resting
   there would resolve to nothing, dropping the highlight and making a
   release over a gap spring the item back. A pseudo-element hit-tests as its
   originating cell, so half the gap in each direction closes the seams with
   no arithmetic. Adjacent areas overlap by a pixel or two; elementFromPoint
   takes the later cell in DOM order, which is deterministic. */
.home-grid-cell::before {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  inset: -4px;
}
/* The cells the drop would land in. A widget tints its whole footprint, so
   "this doesn't fit here" is legible before releasing; an ILLEGAL cell is
   left plain rather than tinted red — the absence of the affordance is the
   clearer signal, and it matches the ghost springing back. */
.home-grid-cell--on {
  background-color: rgba(31, 134, 255, 0.14);
  border-color: rgba(31, 134, 255, 0.75);
  border-style: solid;
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .home-grid-cell { transition: none; }
}
/* Reduce motion setting (#6510): the same, when Settings asks for it. */
:where(html[data-motion="reduce"]) .home-grid-cell { transition: none; }

/* ── The live displacement preview ──────────────────────────
   While something hovers a target, the items it WOULD push move to the cells
   they'd land in. The flow reorder this replaced gave that away for free
   (everything shuffled as you dragged); free placement has to show it
   deliberately, because only the items that actually collide move at all —
   without it a drop onto an occupied cell is a guess.

   The transform is set inline from the plan (a measured delta between two of
   the overlay's own cells); this rule only supplies the MOTION, so it matches
   the kit's FLIP family — 200ms ease, the same as attachReorder's gridFlip.
   z-index keeps a sliding tile above its stationary neighbours instead of
   disappearing behind the one it is passing. */
.home-item-displaced {
  transition: transform 200ms ease;
  z-index: 2;
}

/* Pushed clean off the canvas into the dense overflow rows below it. There is
   no overlay cell to slide to, so the tile says so where it stands: dimmed,
   with a downward chevron. Better than sliding it to a position that does not
   exist yet, and better than moving it silently. */
.home-item-to-overflow {
  position: relative;
  opacity: 0.45;
}
.home-item-to-overflow::after {
  content: '↓';
  position: absolute;
  inset-inline-end: 0.25rem;
  bottom: 0.25rem;
  width: 1.125rem;
  height: 1.125rem;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  border-radius: 9999px;
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  line-height: 1;
  color: #fff;
  background-color: rgba(31, 134, 255, 0.9);
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  /* The new POSITION is the information, so the tiles still move — only the
     tween goes. */
  .home-item-displaced { transition: none; }
}
/* Reduce motion setting (#6510): the same, when Settings asks for it. */
:where(html[data-motion="reduce"]) .home-item-displaced { transition: none; }

/* Tiles sit ABOVE the overlay. Without this the overlay's cells would paint
   over the icons they are supposed to sit behind. */
#app-list > .app-card {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 1;
}

/* ...but they must be TRANSPARENT TO HIT-TESTING for the duration of a drag.
   This is load-bearing, not a polish detail. The overlay's cells are what
   Home._cellFromPoint resolves a pointer to, and they sit BELOW the tiles
   (they have to — a dashed outline painted over an app icon would be
   nonsense). So while the tiles keep their pointer events, elementFromPoint
   under the finger returns a TILE for every occupied cell, cellFromPoint
   returns null, and the drop is treated as "not over the grid":

     • dropping onto another app did nothing at all — the target was never
       even resolvable, so displacement could not be reached;
     • and a tile could not be moved to any position overlapping its OWN
       footprint, because its own drop slot swallowed the hit-test.

   The kit puts .un-reordering on the list for exactly the span of a lift, so
   scoping it there means normal taps are untouched. */
#app-list.un-reordering > .app-card {
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* ── THE DISCOVER LANE IS RETIRED (One Home, #6361) ───────────
   Home's Discover section, its rail of cards, its empty note and its tile
   grid before that went with it: Communities to join is the directory now.
   The card itself (`.home-discover-card` and its art, below) stays, as the
   featured-illustration editor's preview. */
/* ── THE CARD LANGUAGE: TINTS ───────────────────────────────
   Discover's app cards and Challenges' challenge cards are the same object
   in two sizes — a tinted plate with art on it — and they share one palette
   so the launcher reads as one page rather than two lists that happened to
   get colours. Five tints, assigned by a hash of the app slug / challenge id
   (see tintOf in panels/ui.tsx) rather than by position, so a card keeps its
   colour as the lane reorders around it.

   Raw hex, like the wallpaper above and for the same reason: these are the
   design's own tints, not steps of a Tailwind scale, and `zinc`/`violet` are
   the only scales the product is allowed to name (see the palette rule in
   tests/admin-ui-registry.test.js). `--tint-art` is the deeper block the
   icon sits on; `--tint-line` is the hairline that keeps a pale card off a
   pale wallpaper.

   Dark mode does what the wallpaper does: the same hues, re-tuned to a
   lightness that reads on the inverted ground rather than inverted into
   mud. */
.home-tint-1, .home-tint-1[data-preview-theme="light"], .dark .home-tint-1[data-preview-theme="light"] { --tint-bg: #e3efe0; --tint-line: #cfe0ca; --tint-art: #cfe3c7; }
.home-tint-2, .home-tint-2[data-preview-theme="light"], .dark .home-tint-2[data-preview-theme="light"] { --tint-bg: #dfeaf6; --tint-line: #c9dcee; --tint-art: #c6dcf0; }
.home-tint-3, .home-tint-3[data-preview-theme="light"], .dark .home-tint-3[data-preview-theme="light"] { --tint-bg: #e9e2f2; --tint-line: #d9cfe8; --tint-art: #ded2ef; }
.home-tint-4, .home-tint-4[data-preview-theme="light"], .dark .home-tint-4[data-preview-theme="light"] { --tint-bg: #fbe6d5; --tint-line: #f2d5bd; --tint-art: #f7ddc6; }
.home-tint-5, .home-tint-5[data-preview-theme="light"], .dark .home-tint-5[data-preview-theme="light"] { --tint-bg: #f8dde8; --tint-line: #eec7d6; --tint-art: #f4d6de; }
.dark .home-tint-1, .home-tint-1[data-preview-theme="dark"], .dark .home-tint-1[data-preview-theme="dark"] { --tint-bg: #1b2a1f; --tint-line: #2c4433; --tint-art: #22331f; }
.dark .home-tint-2, .home-tint-2[data-preview-theme="dark"], .dark .home-tint-2[data-preview-theme="dark"] { --tint-bg: #17263a; --tint-line: #27405e; --tint-art: #1b2f47; }
.dark .home-tint-3, .home-tint-3[data-preview-theme="dark"], .dark .home-tint-3[data-preview-theme="dark"] { --tint-bg: #241f38; --tint-line: #3a3157; --tint-art: #2b2442; }
.dark .home-tint-4, .home-tint-4[data-preview-theme="dark"], .dark .home-tint-4[data-preview-theme="dark"] { --tint-bg: #33231a; --tint-line: #50392a; --tint-art: #3d2a1f; }
.dark .home-tint-5, .home-tint-5[data-preview-theme="dark"], .dark .home-tint-5[data-preview-theme="dark"] { --tint-bg: #331d28; --tint-line: #513043; --tint-art: #3e2231; }

/* Harmonic palette: select tone 50 once. Dark surfaces are computed from
   that same token, blended into the shell's zinc-950 ground. The fixed 10%,
   14%, 24% proportions give every hue the same quiet surface hierarchy.
   These formulas also drive explicit editor previews; no second palette. */
.home-tone-cream { --tone-50: #fefdf5; --tone-light-line: #f5f0d0; }
.home-tone-yellow { --tone-50: #fff9de; --tone-light-line: #f0d85c; }
.home-tone-orange { --tone-50: #fff0e0; --tone-light-line: #e8a878; }
.home-tone-coral { --tone-50: #fde8e4; --tone-light-line: #e09488; }
.home-tone-pink { --tone-50: #f8e4ee; --tone-light-line: #d48eb4; }
.home-tone-purple { --tone-50: #ede4f4; --tone-light-line: #aa92c2; }
.home-tone-indigo { --tone-50: #e4e8f6; --tone-light-line: #8896c8; }
.home-tone-blue { --tone-50: #deedf8; --tone-light-line: #6aadd4; }
.home-tone-teal { --tone-50: #deeeed; --tone-light-line: #72bab2; }
.home-tone-mint { --tone-50: #e2f2e8; --tone-light-line: #88c49e; }
.home-tone-sage { --tone-50: #eaf2e4; --tone-light-line: #a9cf97; }
.home-tone-gray { --tone-50: #f4f3f1; --tone-light-line: #b8b4ac; }
:is(.home-tone-cream, .home-tone-yellow, .home-tone-orange, .home-tone-coral, .home-tone-pink, .home-tone-purple, .home-tone-indigo, .home-tone-blue, .home-tone-teal, .home-tone-mint, .home-tone-sage, .home-tone-gray) {
  --tone-dark-bg: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tone-50) 10%, #0b0b0c);
  --tone-dark-art: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tone-50) 14%, #0b0b0c);
  --tone-dark-line: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tone-50) 24%, #0b0b0c);
  --tint-bg: var(--tone-50); --tint-line: var(--tone-light-line); --tint-art: var(--tone-50);
}
.dark :is(.home-tone-cream, .home-tone-yellow, .home-tone-orange, .home-tone-coral, .home-tone-pink, .home-tone-purple, .home-tone-indigo, .home-tone-blue, .home-tone-teal, .home-tone-mint, .home-tone-sage, .home-tone-gray), :is(.home-tone-cream, .home-tone-yellow, .home-tone-orange, .home-tone-coral, .home-tone-pink, .home-tone-purple, .home-tone-indigo, .home-tone-blue, .home-tone-teal, .home-tone-mint, .home-tone-sage, .home-tone-gray)[data-preview-theme="dark"] {
  --tint-bg: var(--tone-dark-bg); --tint-line: var(--tone-dark-line); --tint-art: var(--tone-dark-art);
}
.dark :is(.home-tone-cream, .home-tone-yellow, .home-tone-orange, .home-tone-coral, .home-tone-pink, .home-tone-purple, .home-tone-indigo, .home-tone-blue, .home-tone-teal, .home-tone-mint, .home-tone-sage, .home-tone-gray)[data-preview-theme="light"], :is(.home-tone-cream, .home-tone-yellow, .home-tone-orange, .home-tone-coral, .home-tone-pink, .home-tone-purple, .home-tone-indigo, .home-tone-blue, .home-tone-teal, .home-tone-mint, .home-tone-sage, .home-tone-gray)[data-preview-theme="light"] {
  --tint-bg: var(--tone-50); --tint-line: var(--tone-light-line); --tint-art: var(--tone-50);
}

/* The card was enlarged for #6012, from 9.5rem to 11.25rem, so the artwork
   is easier to see; it is still a fixed width on purpose: cards that flex
   would resize as the lane's count changes, and the rail's whole affordance
   is that the row continues past the edge at a constant rhythm. 11.25rem is
   as wide as it goes and still leave a sliver of the frame at a 390px phone:
   the lead padding and the gap are 0.75rem and 0.625rem, so two cards come
   to 12 + 180 + 10 + 180 = 382px and the third starts 2px past the edge,
   where 11.5rem would end the second card flush against it. Narrower phones
   cut the second card instead, which is the stronger cue. */
.home-discover-card {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  width: 11.25rem;
  scroll-snap-align: start;
  border-radius: 0.875rem;
  background: var(--tint-bg);
  border: 1px solid var(--tint-line);
  overflow: hidden;
  cursor: pointer;
}
/* `cover`, not `contain`: the framing editor's zoom bottoms out at the exact
   cover fit and its pan is clamped to it, so the art fills the block at every
   legal framing rather than letterboxing itself against the tint. */
.home-discover-illustration { width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; pointer-events: none; }
.home-discover-art {
  overflow: hidden;
  /* Keeps the block's own ratio with the wider card (#6012): 180 × 120 / 152
     ≈ 142px, so illustration framings are not cropped differently. */
  height: 8.875rem;
  background: var(--tint-art);
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}
/* The app's own icon, at the size the art block can carry. An emoji is a
   glyph and scales with font-size; an uploaded image fills the block, which
   is the one case that looks like the reference's own art. */
.home-discover-art-emoji { font-size: 3.25rem; line-height: 1; }
.home-discover-art-img { width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; }
.home-discover-art-letter { font-size: 2.75rem; line-height: 1; font-weight: 700; opacity: .45; }
/* Two lines, ellipsised — the reference's own treatment. `line-clamp` is
   three properties and one of them is prefixed, which is why it is here
   rather than as utilities beside the element. */
.home-discover-blurb {
  display: -webkit-box;
  -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
  -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
  line-clamp: 2;
  overflow: hidden;
}
/* The same two lines for the NAME (#4937): the single `truncate` it carried
   cut long community names mid-word on a card wide enough to read them.
   `overflow-wrap: anywhere` keeps a name with no spaces in it from
   overflowing the card instead of wrapping. */
.home-discover-name {
  display: -webkit-box;
  -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
  -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
  line-clamp: 2;
  overflow: hidden;
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}

/* ── Challenges: a flat column of cards ─────────────────────
   The block was one white card of divided rows, then a translucent PLATE
   (`.home-challenges-plate`) holding the season summary and the cards. Both
   are gone. The ITERATION 03 board's Home screen draws the area straight on
   the page ground: season summary, group headers, cards and footer in one
   column, 14px apart, aligned with the section heading and with no border or
   rule anywhere. The cards and the group headers are surfaces of their own,
   so the plate was a second frame around them, and its padding pulled the
   block in from the heading's edge. The spacing is utilities in
   frontend/src/features/home/panels/challenges.tsx; nothing here draws it.

   The cards are posters (WipChallengePosters in that file), drawn by the
   `.wip-poster` rules. The tinted card, the white pill, the count capsule and
   the category well that lived here are retired with the design they drew. */

/* ── The Create tile: the launcher grid's last cell ─────────────
   "Create an app" is the grid's trailing tile (the prototype's scrHome), drawn
   entirely in utilities in frontend/src/features/home/create-tile.tsx: an app
   card's cell with a dashed, unfilled face and a muted label. It replaced the
   full-width Create section that sat below Challenges, and the
   `.home-create-widget` wrapper went with that section.

   Here are only the three things a utility cannot say, and each mirrors a rule
   the app cards already carry (the `#app-list > .app-card` rules): the tile
   sits ABOVE the drag overlay, at the top of its row, and it is transparent to
   hit-testing for the span of a lift. That last one is load-bearing for the
   same reason it is on the cards — the overlay cell under the tile has to stay
   a drop target, and a drop there is an ordinary drop into an empty cell (the
   tile is not in the layout; the next paint moves it along). It also fades
   while a drag is live: it is the one thing on the canvas that does not move
   with the preview, and at full strength it read as a tile being displaced. */
#app-list > .home-create-tile {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 1;
  align-self: start;
}
#app-list.un-reordering > .home-create-tile {
  pointer-events: none;
  opacity: 0.4;
}
/* Keyboard focus draws the ring the challenge cards draw
   (`.tc-se-card[role="button"]:focus-visible`). */
.home-create-btn:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

/* ── EDIT MODE (#4669): the wiggle ───────────────────────────
   A held tile puts My apps into edit mode, and the tiles gently wiggle the
   way a phone's home screen does. The animation is on the ICON WRAPPER
   (the card's `.relative` child), not on the card itself: the kit's drag
   writes `transform` to the CARD element for the lift, displacement preview
   and release spring, and an animation transform on the same element would
   fight it. The wrapper is React's alone.

   `alternate` swings the one keyframe pair between -1.5deg and 1.5deg, and
   the per-row delays (2n / 3n) spread the phase so the grid reads as a
   jelly of tiles rather than one rigid tile repeated. */
@keyframes home-wiggle {
  from { transform: rotate(-1.5deg); }
  to { transform: rotate(1.5deg); }
}
#app-list[data-editing] > .app-card > .relative {
  animation: home-wiggle 260ms ease-in-out infinite alternate;
}
#app-list[data-editing] > .app-card:nth-child(2n) > .relative {
  animation-delay: -130ms;
}
#app-list[data-editing] > .app-card:nth-child(3n) > .relative {
  animation-delay: -65ms;
}
/* While a drag is live the kit puts .un-reordering on the list; the wiggle
   pauses so the dragged tile's wrapper is not also turning under the finger. */
#app-list.un-reordering[data-editing] > .app-card > .relative {
  animation-play-state: paused;
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  /* The arrangement affordances stay — the remove buttons, "Done" and the
     Add apps tile are markup, not motion — only the wiggle goes. */
  #app-list[data-editing] > .app-card > .relative {
    animation: none;
  }
}
/* Reduce motion setting (#6510): the same, when Settings asks for it. */
:where(html[data-motion="reduce"]) #app-list[data-editing] > .app-card > .relative { animation: none; }

/* ── Phone density ──────────────────────────────────────────
   Below 640px the grid is 4 columns with a tighter tile, so the cell is 8px
   shorter (p-2 instead of p-3) and the gap is 6px instead of 8, and the
   overlay's inset/gap follows from that — kept here, in one block, so the
   two can't drift.

   640px IS the breakpoint: it is Tailwind's `sm` on #app-list's
   `grid-cols-4 sm:grid-cols-5`, and HomeLayout.BREAKPOINT_PX in
   frontend/src/features/home/home-layout.js. All three must agree or the JS
   lays out against a column count the CSS isn't rendering. */
@media (max-width: 639.98px) {
  :root {
    /* 116px — p-2 (16) + icon 56 + gap-1.5 (6) + title 26 + caption 12 */
    --home-cell-h: 7.25rem;
    /* --home-panel-max-h was redefined here as ONE CELL (#968), because a
       block was a grid item whose phone footprint was a single row and a
       238px cap in a 116px cell would have let an oversized system text size
       paint the article over the app tiles below it. The blocks are fixed
       sections outside the grid now — they overlap nothing and they size to
       their own content — so neither the override nor the token it narrowed
       exists any more. */
  }
  .home-grid-overlay { gap: 0.375rem; }
  /* The doubled class is deliberate. This rule is what makes the phone
     cell 116px rather than 124px, but the card element carries Tailwind's
     own `p-3`, and tailwind.css is linked AFTER app.css in index.html — so
     at equal specificity the utility won and this tightening never applied
     (the cell budget above said p-2 while tiles rendered at p-3, which is
     how a captioned tile came to overflow its row). `.app-card.app-card`
     is the same element, one specificity step up, so the intended phone
     padding lands without an !important or a #app-list scope that would
     miss the landing grid's tiles. It also buys the label 8px of extra
     line width, which at 11px is roughly two more characters per line.

     NOT the Discover card. It carries `.app-card` only as the wiring contract
     with `_wireDiscoveryCards`; it is a tile of its own whose art block is
     meant to run edge to edge, and this padding inset the illustration inside
     a frame of tint on every phone. */
  .app-card.app-card:not(.home-discover-card) { padding: 0.5rem; }

  /* `.home-panel-slot > .home-panel--fit { align-self: flex-start }` lived
     here: the phone-only release from the slot's stretch-to-fill, so a short
     block ended sooner instead of spending its cell on whitespace. Both the
     slot and the stretch went with the placement — a section is as tall as it
     draws, everywhere, with no rule needed to say so. */
}
@media (min-width: 640px) {
  .home-grid-overlay {
    /* #app-list's sm:p-3 sm:pt-2 */
    inset: 0.5rem 0.75rem 0.75rem;
    gap: 0.5rem;      /* sm:gap-2 */
  }
  /* THE MY APPS BLOCK'S WIDTH (#4861): the area spans the full column
     again. #4738 had capped it at 48rem and centred it, so a few apps
     grouped closer — but on review of that softening the narrower block
     read as odder than wide spacing: the grid sat inside a box narrower
     than the search bar above it and the sections below (evan, on #4861),
     so the cap is gone and everything on Home lines up at the same edges
     again. Tile padding and gaps are untouched, so the overlay's inset
     below still mirrors #app-list, and the cap being on no box at all means
     the absolutely positioned drag overlay (inside #app-list) and Home's
     cell measurements still line up with zero arithmetic. Search keeps the
     full width it always had. */
}

/* App tiles sit at the TOP of their row rather than stretching to fill it.
   Rows are a fixed --home-cell-h whenever tiles alone define them, so this
   changes nothing on its own. It earned its keep in the rows a multi-cell
   WIDGET spanned, where an expanded block made the row taller than a card and
   a stretched tile grew a pool of dead space under its title; nothing spans
   rows since THE UI OVERHAUL. It stays because the blank rows
   Home.rowTemplate emits (#975) are half a cell, and a tile stretched to a
   short track is the same wrong default in the other direction. */
#app-list > .app-card {
  align-self: start;
}

/* The feed body is a full-height flex COLUMN so the trailing sections can
   be bottom-anchored on a tall screen.

   min-height:100% (of the #home-screen scroller) does double duty:
   - it guarantees the scroller can always be scrolled by at least the
     hidden search bar's height, even with an empty "Your apps" — a short
     page would otherwise leave the bar permanently on screen (the search
     bar sits ABOVE this box inside the same scroller, so total content is
     always barHeight + 100%, and Home._searchReveal parks the scroller at
     barHeight);
   - it makes this box exactly as tall as the visible feed at that resting
     scroll position, which is what makes "anchored to the bottom of the
     box" read as "anchored to the bottom of the page".

   Height stays AUTO (min-height is only a floor), so nothing shrinks and
   nothing clips once the content is taller than the viewport. The
   safe-area padding keeps a bottom-anchored section off an iPhone's home
   indicator; box-sizing:border-box means it eats into the 100% rather
   than adding scroll. */
.home-body-fill {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  min-height: 100%;
  /* #home-screen is the one screen root with no `.platform-safe-scroll`, so
     it reserves the tab bar's band here instead — same `max()`, same reason
     (see that utility near the top of this file). It matters more here than
     anywhere else: the trailing sections are BOTTOM-ANCHORED to this box, so
     under-reserving does not merely crop the last card, it parks Create and
     Challenges permanently behind the bar. */
  padding-bottom: max(var(--platform-tabs-h, 0px), var(--platform-safe-bottom));
  /* 4px, so the first label (its own `pt-4`) clears the header's notch by
     the 12px Workshop, Messages and Profile leave: the bar overlaps this
     box by 8. It sits on #home-body, not the search bar, because the bar
     parks by its own offsetHeight (home.js), and a taller bar would park
     the gap away with it. */
  padding-top: 4px;
}
html[data-browser-scroller="home-screen"] .home-body-fill {
  /* The header overlaps its content by .5rem (its rounded lower edge). */
  min-height: calc(100dvh - var(--browser-banner-h) - var(--platform-header-h) - var(--platform-safe-top) + .5rem);
}

/* The Workshop front page's three dots, travelling its path from the start
   to the end (the standard look's block below, "A project's Workshop: its
   front page"): a keyframe is no rule of a look, so it stands outside it. */
@keyframes ws-front-run {
  0% { inset-inline-start: 2%; opacity: 0; }
  8% { opacity: 1; }
  92% { opacity: 1; }
  100% { inset-inline-start: 98%; opacity: 0; }
}
/* ============================================================
   STANDARD (WIP): the standard look's own kit. It began as Standard (WIP),
   "Homeroom brand kit (WIP)", a look where changes to the standard one
   were tried first (data-skin-wip on <html>). The standard look took them
   on 20 Nov 2026 and the WIP look was removed on 24 Nov 2026; its `--wip-*`
   tokens and `.wip-*` classes kept their names.

   THE RULES OF THIS BLOCK (tests/standard-wip-look.test.js holds them):
     1. Every value is a token. The `--wip-*` tokens are declared once, in
        the first rule, from the shell's own tokens where one exists; no
        rule after it writes a colour of its own.
     2. Every rule is scoped to `html:root` or is a `.wip-*` component
        class (features/home/wip-kit.tsx). `html:root` (here and across
        this sheet) is what `html:not([data-skin])` became when the other
        looks were removed (26 Nov 2026): it always matches, and it weighs
        what the :not() weighed, so every rule wins and loses as it did.
     3. Nothing of the standard look is hidden here except what WIP_HIDES
        in that test lists, so a feature added to Standard shows under
        this look too unless somebody decides otherwise there.
   ============================================================ */
html:root {
  /* The page: its ground and its typeface. This config leaves both to the
     shell; another config may name its own. */
  --wip-page-bg: transparent;
  --wip-font: inherit;
  /* A project's hub: its own icon (a Home tile's, a size up), its name and its head card's padding. */
  --wip-hub-icon: 2.75rem;
  --wip-hub-name: 1.625rem;
  --wip-hub-name-font: inherit;
  --wip-hub-name-weight: 800;
  --wip-hub-pad: 1.25rem;
  /* The hub's head is a hero: taller, with more room round it, and its
     ground starts in the community's own colour and fades to the card's. */
  --wip-ghost: color-mix(in srgb, var(--wip-ink) 8%, transparent); /* under a quiet icon button the pointer is on */
  --wip-face-ink: #ffffff;   /* an initial on a member's colour */
  --wip-menu-shadow: 0 18px 40px -16px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.28), 0 4px 10px -6px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.14); /* a menu's drop */
  /* A door's tile (the hub's Needs you, Workshop, Discussion): the look's own mark colours, the count's. */
  --wip-door-tile-bg: var(--wip-count-bg);
  --wip-door-tile-ink: var(--wip-count-ink);
  /* Three marks a set of icons takes turns in, so a row of tiles is not one
     colour: the accent, the "going well" green and the "look here" amber of
     the shell here; a config names its own three. */
  --wip-mark-1-bg: var(--accent-tint);
  --wip-mark-1-ink: var(--accent-tint-ink);
  --wip-mark-2-bg: var(--state-ok-bg);
  --wip-mark-2-ink: var(--state-ok);
  --wip-mark-3-bg: var(--state-attention-bg);
  --wip-mark-3-ink: var(--state-attention);
  /* A status tag on a board card, by what it says: plain, going well, wrong,
     look here, and under way. Wrong and look-here keep the shell's own state
     colours at full strength, so a failing check is never a quiet thing. */
  --wip-tag-plain-bg: var(--wip-ghost);
  --wip-tag-plain-ink: var(--wip-ink-soft);
  --wip-tag-ok-bg: var(--wip-mark-3-bg);
  --wip-tag-ok-ink: var(--wip-ok);
  --wip-tag-bad-bg: var(--state-blocked-bg);
  --wip-tag-bad-ink: var(--state-blocked);
  --wip-tag-warn-bg: var(--state-attention-bg);
  --wip-tag-warn-ink: var(--state-attention);
  --wip-tag-run-bg: var(--wip-mark-1-bg);
  --wip-tag-run-ink: var(--wip-mark-1-ink);
  --wip-tray: color-mix(in srgb, var(--wip-ink) 5%, transparent); /* a column's ground on the board: a soft tint the cards sit on */
  /* A very soft card inside a card (a community on the Communities page): a faint tint of the ink and no edge; a step deeper under the pointer. */
  --wip-soft-bg: color-mix(in srgb, var(--wip-ink) 3%, transparent);
  --wip-soft-bg-on: color-mix(in srgb, var(--wip-ink) 6%, transparent);
  /* The Communities page's own ground: the page's unless a config names another. */
  --wip-page-bg-communities: var(--wip-page-bg);
  --wip-soft-line: color-mix(in srgb, var(--wip-ink) 6%, transparent); /* a rule inside a card (over a community's week): softer than the card's hairline */
  --wip-card-line-hover: color-mix(in srgb, var(--wip-ink) 15%, transparent); /* a card's edge under the pointer: a shade deeper, not the ink */
  --wip-scrim: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.62); /* over a card while a sheet on the card beside it is up: the vote's own dim (.rf-scrim), so the two cards dim alike */
  --wip-thumb: 6rem;         /* a change's picture in a list: a thumbnail at its side */
  --wip-hero-pad: 1.25rem;
  --wip-hero-min: 0;        /* a poster's height, whatever it says */
  --wip-hero-art: 4.5rem;       /* the icon at its side */
  /* The hero wears the community's colour softly: its HUE, at a lightness
     and strength the look chooses, so a navy or a charcoal project gets as
     airy a card as an orange one. A pale ground, three drifting washes of
     the hue and its two neighbours, and the action in the hue itself. */
  --wip-hero-tint: var(--community-tint, #3b5bdb);
  --wip-hero-bg: color-mix(in srgb, hsl(from var(--wip-hero-tint) h 60% 60%) 5%, var(--wip-card-bg));
  --wip-hero-wash-a: hsl(from var(--wip-hero-tint) h 70% 80% / 0.38);
  --wip-hero-wash-b: hsl(from var(--wip-hero-tint) calc(h + 40) 70% 84% / 0.3);
  --wip-hero-wash-c: hsl(from var(--wip-hero-tint) calc(h - 30) 70% 86% / 0.28);
  --wip-hero-line: var(--wip-card-line);
  --wip-hero-ink: var(--wip-ink);
  --wip-hero-ink-soft: var(--wip-ink-soft);
  --wip-hero-ink-muted: var(--wip-ink-muted);
  --wip-hero-glass: var(--wip-card-bg);    /* a quiet control on the card */
  --wip-hero-glass-line: var(--wip-card-line);
  --wip-hero-ring: hsl(from var(--wip-hero-tint) h 40% 50% / 0.1);
  --wip-hero-dot: hsl(from var(--wip-hero-tint) h 30% 40% / 0.1);
  --wip-hero-shadow: var(--wip-card-shadow);
  --wip-hero-art-shadow: 0 18px 34px -18px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.3), 0 0 0 1px var(--wip-card-line);
  --wip-hero-dots-mask: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent 30%, #000000 75%);   /* the dots thin out toward the words */
  --wip-hero-rings-mask: radial-gradient(closest-side, #000000 30%, transparent); /* the rings fade as they spread */
  --wip-hero-drift: 28s;        /* one slow turn of the washes */
  --wip-hero-float: 7s;         /* the picture's rise and fall */
  /* A section's label under a token config: its face, size, case, spacing,
     weight and ink. These are the shell's own label's values, so a config
     that does not name them draws the label as it is. */
  --wip-label-font: inherit;
  --wip-label-size: 0.75rem;
  --wip-label-case: uppercase;
  --wip-label-track: 0.06em;
  --wip-label-weight: 700;
  --wip-label-ink: var(--wip-ink-muted);
  /* A highlighter's stroke through the lower half of a label's words: none unless a config names its colour. */
  --wip-label-mark: transparent;
  /* What a challenge is worth: plain words unless a config gives them a ground. */
  --wip-worth-bg: transparent;
  --wip-worth-ink: var(--wip-ink);
  /* A card that asks something of the viewer (Needs you) may wear its own
     colours. By default they are the card's. */
  --wip-ask-bg: var(--wip-card-bg);
  --wip-ask-line: var(--wip-card-line);
  --wip-ask-ink: var(--wip-ink);
  --wip-ask-ink-muted: var(--wip-ink-muted);
  --wip-ask-pill-bg: var(--wip-pill-bg);
  --wip-ask-pill-ink: var(--wip-pill-ink);
  /* A card of things to aim for (Challenges) may wear its own colours too:
     its ground and edge, its words, its meter, and how much of a picture's
     tone its band wears. By default they are the card's. */
  --wip-goal-bg: var(--wip-card-bg);
  --wip-goal-line: var(--wip-card-line);
  --wip-goal-ink: var(--wip-ink);
  --wip-goal-ink-muted: var(--wip-ink-muted);
  --wip-goal-meter: var(--wip-meter);
  --wip-goal-poster-tone: var(--wip-poster-tone);
  --wip-goal-poster-deep: var(--wip-poster-deep);
  /* The soft disc under a title's chevron when the pointer is on the title */
  --wip-head-disc: var(--wip-count-bg);
  /* A card's lift off the page: none here, where its edge is a hairline. */
  --wip-card-shadow: 0 0 transparent;
  /* A placeholder's fill: a tenth of the ink, on whatever it sits on */
  --wip-skeleton: color-mix(in srgb, var(--wip-ink) 10%, transparent);
  /* Space */
  --wip-gutter: 12px;          /* the page's side gutter, a section's px-3 */
  --wip-row-gap: 2rem;         /* between Home's rows */
  --wip-card-gap: 1rem;        /* between two cards of one row */
  --wip-card-pad: 12px;
  --wip-col-gap: 0.5rem;       /* between two columns of a list: the projects grid's own gap, so every list's columns fall on the same lines */
  --wip-cell-pad: 0.75rem;     /* inside a column, before its icon: a project tile's own padding */
  /* Corners: three steps and the pill. A card; what sits inside a card (an
     icon, an inner card, a field); a small mark (a count, a status). The
     pill is for what is pressed (a button, a chip) and nothing else. An
     inner corner is always tighter than the one round it. */
  --wip-radius-card: 16px;
  --wip-radius-inner: 10px;
  --wip-radius-small: 6px;
  --wip-radius-pill: 9999px;
  /* The card */
  --wip-card-bg: var(--dc-sheet-solid);
  --wip-card-line: var(--app-sheet-line);
  --wip-card-radius: var(--wip-radius-card);
  /* Type: a row is 15 over 12, with an 11px line of facts */
  --wip-title-size: 0.9375rem;
  --wip-title-weight: 650;
  --wip-body-size: 0.75rem;
  --wip-meta-size: 0.6875rem;
  --wip-link-size: 0.875rem;
  --wip-ink-muted: var(--text-muted);
  --wip-ink-action: var(--accent);
  /* A project's tile */
  --wip-tile-h: 6rem;          /* p-3 12 + icon 72 + p-3 12: the tile's own 96px */
  --wip-title-size-phone: 0.6875rem; /* a project's name under its icon on a phone */
  --wip-title-size-mid: 0.75rem;    /* and on a tablet, where it is still under the icon */
  --wip-title-gap-stacked: 0.375rem; /* the air between the icon and the name under it */
  --wip-tile-h-stacked: 7.625rem; /* under 1024px: the tile's own 122px (icon, gap, two lines of the name, its padding), nothing spare */
  --wip-tile-h-phone: 6.875rem;  /* and on a phone, where the name is a size down: 110px */
  --wip-tile-gap: 0.75rem;
  --wip-icon: 4.5rem;
  --wip-icon-radius: 1.125rem;  /* 18px: an icon is rounder than the things beside it, a quarter of its side, as a phone's app icon is */
  --wip-icon-glyph: 2rem;      /* an emoji icon's size */
  --wip-icon-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.06), 0 6px 14px -4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2);
  --wip-icon-edge: var(--wip-card-line); /* a hairline round an icon's tile, so a white icon keeps its shape on a white card */
  --wip-icon-tile: var(--bg-primary); /* the tile under an emoji or a letter */
  /* The filled action */
  --wip-cta-h: 2.5rem;
  --wip-cta-from: #5aa9ff;
  --wip-cta-via: var(--accent);
  --wip-cta-to: #0062cc;
  --wip-cta-ink: #ffffff;
  --wip-cta-glow: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 40%, transparent);
  /* The button on a label that folds its section: a quiet pill on the card's colour unless a config draws it its own way. */
  --wip-fold-h: 1.625rem;
  --wip-fold-bg: var(--wip-card-bg);
  --wip-fold-bg-on: var(--wip-card-bg);
  --wip-fold-ink: var(--wip-ink);
  --wip-fold-edge: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--wip-card-line);
  --wip-fold-edge-down: var(--wip-fold-edge);
  --wip-fold-press: scale(0.98);
  /* The page's own top action (New project on Home, New community on Communities) is the filled action unless a config draws it its own way. */
  --wip-top-cta-bg: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--wip-cta-from), var(--wip-cta-via) 50%, var(--wip-cta-to));
  --wip-top-cta-ink: var(--wip-cta-ink);
  --wip-top-cta-weight: 600;
  --wip-top-cta-edge: 0 10px 15px -3px var(--wip-cta-glow), 0 4px 6px -4px var(--wip-cta-glow), inset 0 0 0 1px rgb(255 255 255 / 0.2);
  --wip-top-cta-edge-down: var(--wip-top-cta-edge);
  --wip-top-cta-lift: translateY(-1px);
  --wip-top-cta-press: scale(0.98);
  /* A panel's row of items (Needs you) */
  --wip-item-radius: var(--wip-radius-inner);
  /* Its ground under the pointer, quiet because such a row is something to
     open, not something asked of the viewer; a shade deeper while pressed. */
  --wip-item-hover: color-mix(in srgb, var(--wip-ink) 5%, transparent);
  --wip-item-press: color-mix(in srgb, var(--wip-ink) 9%, transparent);
  /* A pinned tile's scene: the band's height, and the brand kit's six backgrounds it takes in turn. */
  --wip-scene-band: 4.75rem;
  --wip-scene-1: url('/brand/bg/sky.webp');
  --wip-scene-2: url('/brand/bg/halftone.webp');
  --wip-scene-3: url('/brand/bg/dream.webp');
  --wip-scene-4: url('/brand/bg/stars.webp');
  --wip-scene-5: url('/brand/bg/collage.webp');
  --wip-scene-6: url('/brand/bg/stripes.webp');
  /* A pinned community's card on the Communities page: the least width a row
     of them gives each. */
  --wip-pin-card: 16.5rem;
  --wip-item-icon: 2.25rem;
  --wip-item-icon-radius: 0.5625rem; /* 9px, the same quarter of a small icon's side */
  --wip-ink: var(--text-primary);
  --wip-ink-soft: var(--text-secondary);
  --wip-pill-bg: var(--accent);
  --wip-pill-ink: var(--wip-cta-ink);
  --wip-count-bg: var(--accent-tint);
  --wip-count-ink: var(--accent-tint-ink);
  /* A challenge picture's band: how much of its pale tone it wears and, under
     that, how much of the tone's deeper line colour over the card. The light
     is all pale tone; the dark is none of it and a third of the deep one,
     since a pastel over black is only grey. */
  --wip-poster-tone: 100%;
  --wip-poster-deep: 0%;
  /* A meter's fill, and a hairline round a count */
  --wip-meter: var(--wip-ink-action);
  --wip-count-line: transparent;
  --wip-poster-art: 4rem;      /* a challenge's picture, a step under a project's icon */
  /* A thing done */
  --wip-ok: var(--state-ok);
  /* Widths */
  --wip-search-w: 24rem;
  --wip-topbar-pad-phone: 1.75rem; /* above the search row on a phone: the header covers its first 8px */
}
/* ── The token set that draws the look ──────────────────────
   The rule above is the list every value starts from. The look is drawn
   with one set of other values over it, the brand kit's, in the rule under
   this comment.
   Three more drawings were tried and removed on 20 Nov 2026: the list
   above on its own, the brand kit's second drawing and Highlighter. Only
   the values differ: no rule of the look knows which set is on.

   BRAND: Homeroom's brand kit (Baked Design Studio, 2026). Cream is the
   page and black the ink; yellow is the filled action and a count, with
   black on it; blue marks what asks for the viewer, also under black (the
   kit's own contrast table: black on blue 6.2:1, white would fail). Type is
   Geist, the kit's typeface for interface text; its serif is for headlines,
   and Home has none. */
html:root {
  /* The kit's three colours as the marks icons take turns in: each a pale
     wash of the colour (a quarter of it, or less, over the card) with the
     glyph in a deep tone of the same colour. */
  --wip-mark-1-bg: rgb(255 238 111 / 0.24);
  --wip-mark-1-ink: #7a6a00;
  --wip-mark-2-bg: rgb(48 144 225 / 0.09);
  --wip-mark-2-ink: #1c64a8;
  --wip-mark-3-bg: rgb(169 207 151 / 0.22);
  --wip-mark-3-ink: #2f6b1e;
  --wip-page-bg: #fffeea;
  --wip-font: 'Geist', system-ui, -apple-system, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif;
  --wip-card-bg: #ffffff;
  --wip-card-line: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.1);
  --wip-ink: #000000;
  --wip-ink-soft: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.72);
  --wip-ink-muted: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.6);
  --wip-ink-action: #000000;
  --wip-icon-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.05), 0 6px 14px -6px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.16);
  /* A field's soft drop under its hairline: the line box, which is white on a white card. */
  --wip-field-drop: 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.06);
  /* The filled action is the kit's Cream + Black: yellow on a cream page
     would be the quietest thing on it. */
  --wip-cta-from: #000000;
  --wip-cta-via: #000000;
  --wip-cta-to: #000000;
  --wip-cta-ink: #fffeea;
  --wip-cta-glow: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.22);
  /* The page's top action is the brand look's sun pill (asked 19 Nov 2026): yellow, an ink outline and a hard edge under it that a press takes up. */
  /* Communities stands on the kit's green, pale (asked 19 Nov 2026): the page is told apart from Home's cream at a glance. */
  --wip-page-bg-communities: #eef5e9;
  --wip-top-cta-bg: #ffee6f;
  --wip-top-cta-ink: #121a0e;
  --wip-top-cta-weight: 700;
  --wip-top-cta-edge: inset 0 0 0 1px #121a0e, 0 2px 0 #121a0e;
  --wip-top-cta-edge-down: inset 0 0 0 1px #121a0e, 0 0 0 #121a0e;
  --wip-top-cta-lift: none;
  --wip-top-cta-press: translateY(2px);
  /* The fold button is the brand look's outlined pill, the sun pill's quiet sibling: white, an ink outline and a hard edge under it; yellow under the pointer. */
  --wip-fold-bg: #ffffff;
  --wip-fold-bg-on: #ffee6f;
  --wip-fold-ink: #121a0e;
  --wip-fold-edge: inset 0 0 0 1px #121a0e, 0 2px 0 #121a0e;
  --wip-fold-edge-down: inset 0 0 0 1px #121a0e, 0 0 0 #121a0e;
  --wip-fold-press: translateY(2px);
  --wip-pill-bg: #3090e1;
  --wip-pill-ink: #000000;
  --wip-count-bg: #ffee6f;
  --wip-count-ink: #000000;
  --wip-count-line: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2);
  --wip-meter: #3090e1;
  /* The shell's own marks on a page of this config: where you are, in the
     kit's ink on a wash of it, not the standard blue. */
  /* The Workshop's front page (24 Nov 2026): the path's four stops, the hub
     hero's three brand colours and the vote's near-black, each with the ink
     its icon wears and a pale halo; the vote pill; the hero's own ground, a
     step between the page and a card; its soft rings, a field's stronger
     hairline, and the lift under the box and the small cards. */
  --wip-path-ask: #ffee6f;
  --wip-path-ask-ink: #121a0e;
  --wip-path-ask-halo: rgb(255 238 111 / 0.28);
  --wip-path-build: #3c7bd8;
  --wip-path-build-ink: #ffffff;
  --wip-path-build-halo: rgb(60 123 216 / 0.14);
  --wip-path-vote: #1c1c1e;
  --wip-path-vote-ink: #ffffff;
  --wip-path-vote-halo: rgb(113 113 122 / 0.14);
  --wip-path-live: #a9cf97;
  --wip-path-live-ink: #1c3a18;
  --wip-path-live-halo: rgb(169 207 151 / 0.26);
  --wip-path-pill-bg: #1c1c1e;
  --wip-path-pill-ink: #ffffff;
  --wip-path-pill-hover: #3f3f46;
  --wip-front-hero-bg: color-mix(in srgb, var(--wip-page-bg) 60%, var(--wip-card-bg));
  --wip-front-ring: rgb(28 28 30 / 0.07);
  --wip-front-line-strong: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.16);
  --wip-front-lift: 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.06), 0 10px 24px -16px rgb(17 24 39 / 0.35);
  --wip-front-mini-shadow: 0 8px 18px -10px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.28);
}
/* In the dark it is the kit's other pairing, cream on black. */
html:root.dark {
  --wip-mark-1-bg: rgb(255 238 111 / 0.16);
  --wip-mark-1-ink: #ffee6f;
  --wip-mark-2-bg: rgb(48 144 225 / 0.2);
  --wip-mark-2-ink: #8cc4ff;
  --wip-mark-3-bg: rgb(169 207 151 / 0.18);
  --wip-mark-3-ink: #a9cf97;
  --wip-page-bg: #0b0b0c; /* the shell's own dark ground, so the page and its frame are one black */
  --wip-card-bg: #171715;
  --wip-card-line: rgb(255 254 234 / 0.14);
  --wip-ink: #fffeea;
  --wip-ink-soft: rgb(255 254 234 / 0.78);
  --wip-ink-muted: rgb(255 254 234 / 0.62);
  --wip-ink-action: #fffeea;
  --wip-icon-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.4), 0 6px 14px -4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.6);
  --wip-field-drop: 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.4);
  /* On black the filled action is the kit's Black + Yellow. */
  --wip-cta-from: #ffee6f;
  --wip-cta-via: #ffee6f;
  --wip-cta-to: #ffee6f;
  --wip-cta-ink: #000000;
  --wip-cta-glow: rgb(255 238 111 / 0.18);
  /* In the dark the sun pill is a shade deeper and has no edge, as the brand look's is. */
  --wip-page-bg-communities: #0c120b; /* the same green, a breath of it on black */
  --wip-top-cta-bg: #f3d920;
  --wip-top-cta-edge: 0 0 0 transparent;
  --wip-top-cta-edge-down: 0 0 0 transparent;
  --wip-fold-bg: #171715;
  --wip-fold-bg-on: #2a2a25; /* a step lighter under the pointer: cream words would not read on the yellow */
  --wip-fold-ink: #fffeea;
  --wip-fold-edge: inset 0 0 0 1px rgb(255 254 234 / 0.4);
  --wip-fold-edge-down: inset 0 0 0 1px rgb(255 254 234 / 0.4);
  --wip-count-line: transparent;
  /* The Workshop's front page: on black the vote's stop and its pill are the kit's cream, with black on it. */
  --wip-path-vote: #fffeea;
  --wip-path-vote-ink: #121a0e;
  --wip-path-vote-halo: rgb(255 254 234 / 0.12);
  --wip-path-build-halo: rgb(60 123 216 / 0.26);
  --wip-path-ask-halo: rgb(255 238 111 / 0.16);
  --wip-path-live-halo: rgb(169 207 151 / 0.18);
  --wip-path-pill-bg: #fffeea;
  --wip-path-pill-ink: #000000;
  --wip-path-pill-hover: rgb(255 254 234 / 0.84);
  --wip-front-ring: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.07);
  --wip-front-line-strong: rgb(255 254 234 / 0.24);
  --wip-front-lift: 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.4), 0 10px 24px -16px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.7);
  --wip-front-mini-shadow: 0 8px 18px -10px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.6);
}
/* How many columns a list of the look has, by the room there is: one, two
   from 640px, and four from 1024px, where Pinned projects' own four columns
   become rows. Needs you's rows read the same number, the same gap and the
   same padding as that grid, so the two lists' columns fall on the same
   lines. Challenges' posters are cards of their own: the same number, a card
   gap apart, so their outer edges fall on those two cards' edges. */
html:root { --wip-cols: 1; }
@media (min-width: 640px) { html:root { --wip-cols: 2; } }
@media (min-width: 1024px) { html:root { --wip-cols: 4; } }
/* In the dark (either config): an icon's tile is a step lighter than the
   card and edged, so a dark picture stays a shape; a challenge's band wears
   a third of its tone's deep colour. */
html:root.dark {
  --wip-poster-tone: 0%;
  --wip-poster-deep: 34%;
  --wip-icon-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.3), 0 6px 14px -4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.55);
  --wip-icon-edge: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.14);
  --wip-icon-tile: #2a2a2d;
}

/* ── Components (features/home/wip-kit.tsx) ─────────────────── */
/* The card. */
.wip-card {
  border-radius: var(--wip-card-radius);
  background-color: var(--wip-card-bg);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--wip-card-line), var(--wip-card-shadow);
}
/* A card that asks something of the viewer wears the config's "ask"
   colours: it re-points the tokens its insides read, so rows, pills and
   words follow without a rule of their own. Under this look only: another
   look that borrows the kit (the brand look sets the kit's tokens from its
   own) has no "ask" colours to point at. */
html:root .wip-card[data-tone="ask"] {
  --wip-card-bg: var(--wip-ask-bg);
  --wip-card-line: var(--wip-ask-line);
  --wip-ink: var(--wip-ask-ink);
  --wip-ink-soft: var(--wip-ask-ink); /* a plain pill's words (Advisory) are the block's own ink, not the page's */
  --wip-ink-muted: var(--wip-ask-ink-muted);
  --wip-ink-action: var(--wip-ask-ink);
  --wip-pill-bg: var(--wip-ask-pill-bg);
  --wip-pill-ink: var(--wip-ask-pill-ink);
}
/* A card of a thing to aim for (each challenge on Home) wears the config's
   "goal" colours, the same way. */
html:root .wip-card[data-tone="goal"] {
  --wip-card-bg: var(--wip-goal-bg);
  --wip-card-line: var(--wip-goal-line);
  --wip-ink: var(--wip-goal-ink);
  --wip-ink-soft: var(--wip-goal-ink);
  --wip-ink-muted: var(--wip-goal-ink-muted);
  --wip-ink-action: var(--wip-goal-ink);
  --wip-meter: var(--wip-goal-meter);
  --wip-poster-tone: var(--wip-goal-poster-tone);
  --wip-poster-deep: var(--wip-goal-poster-deep);
}
/* The page's one filled action. */
.wip-cta {
  display: inline-flex;
  flex: none;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  height: var(--wip-cta-h);
  padding-inline: 1.25rem;
  border-radius: var(--wip-radius-pill);
  background-image: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--wip-cta-from), var(--wip-cta-via) 50%, var(--wip-cta-to));
  color: var(--wip-cta-ink);
  font-size: var(--wip-link-size);
  font-weight: 600;
  box-shadow: 0 10px 15px -3px var(--wip-cta-glow), 0 4px 6px -4px var(--wip-cta-glow), inset 0 0 0 1px rgb(255 255 255 / 0.2);
  transition: transform 150ms, filter 150ms, box-shadow 150ms;
}
.wip-cta:hover { transform: translateY(-1px); filter: brightness(1.1); }
.wip-cta:active { transform: scale(0.98); }
.wip-cta-glyph { width: 1.125rem; height: 1.125rem; }
/* A section's label as the way to all of it: the label's words (a count
   beside them), then a chevron, one thing to press. The words keep the
   label's own type, which it inherits; under the pointer they take the ink
   and the chevron a soft disc. */
.wip-head-more {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.375rem;
  max-width: 100%;
  font: inherit;
  letter-spacing: inherit;
  text-transform: inherit;
  color: inherit;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: color 150ms;
}
/* Under a POINTER only (phone review, 23 Nov 2026): iOS keeps :hover on the
   last thing tapped, so after "Show all" the label stayed near black and its
   underline black, beside a link still in blue, until something else was
   tapped. The same gate is on this link's three other hover rules below. */
@media (hover: hover) { .wip-head-more:hover { color: var(--wip-ink); } }
.wip-head-chevron {
  display: inline-flex;
  flex: none;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: 1.25rem;
  height: 1.25rem;
  margin-inline-start: -0.125rem;
  border-radius: var(--wip-radius-pill);
  color: var(--wip-ink-action);
  transition: background-color 150ms;
}
@media (hover: hover) { .wip-head-more:hover > .wip-head-chevron { background-color: var(--wip-head-disc); } }
.wip-head-chevron-glyph { width: 0.875rem; height: 0.875rem; }
/* A label that folds its section says so on a small button after its words
   (asked 19 Nov 2026): the words of what a press does and the chevron, in
   the config's own outlined pill. The label and the button are one thing to
   press; a press takes the button down onto its edge. */
.wip-head-more:has(> .wip-fold-pill) { gap: 0.625rem; padding-block: 0.1875rem; }
.wip-fold-pill {
  display: inline-flex;
  flex: none;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.25rem;
  height: var(--wip-fold-h);
  padding-inline: 0.625rem 0.5rem;
  border-radius: var(--wip-radius-pill);
  background-color: var(--wip-fold-bg);
  box-shadow: var(--wip-fold-edge);
  font-family: var(--wip-font, inherit);
  font-size: var(--wip-body-size);
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1;
  letter-spacing: normal;
  text-transform: none;
  text-decoration: none;
  color: var(--wip-fold-ink);
  transition: transform 150ms, box-shadow 150ms, background-color 150ms;
}
@media (hover: hover) { .wip-head-more:hover > .wip-fold-pill { background-color: var(--wip-fold-bg-on); } }
.wip-head-more:active > .wip-fold-pill { box-shadow: var(--wip-fold-edge-down); transform: var(--wip-fold-press); }
/* Rows: one thing a line, a hairline between them. */
.wip-rows { display: grid; min-width: 0; margin-top: 0.25rem; overflow: hidden; }
.wip-rows > li { min-width: 0; }
/* The hairline is drawn above each row, and the list clips the first line's,
   so it holds however many columns there are. */
.wip-rows > li { box-shadow: 0 -1px 0 var(--wip-card-line); }
/* In as many columns as the look has room for (--wip-cols). */
.wip-rows-cols { grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--wip-cols, 1), minmax(0, 1fr)); column-gap: var(--wip-col-gap); }
.wip-row {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.75rem;
  min-height: 3.25rem;
  padding: 0.5rem var(--wip-cell-pad);
  border-radius: var(--wip-item-radius);
}
/* Under the pointer a row's title takes the action's ink: that is how the
   brand look's rows answer it, in its sky. In this look's card that asks,
   that ink is the card's own, so the title stays as it is and the ground
   below answers instead. */
.wip-row:hover .wip-item-title { color: var(--wip-ink-action); }
/* A row that opens something answers the pointer, a press and the keyboard.
   A soft ground under the whole row, in its own round corners, and a shade
   deeper while it is pressed, only where a pointer hovers: a tap on a phone
   can leave a row :hover, and the ground would stay on the row it opened.
   A ring for the keyboard, inside the row's edge, since the list clips what
   is drawn outside it. A placeholder on its way is a row too, but not a
   link, so none of this reaches it. This look and the standard one only:
   the brand look sets these rows flush with its card's inset, where a ground
   would start at the icon's edge, and answers with the title's colour (the
   rule above). */
html:root a.wip-row { transition: background-color 120ms ease; }
@media (hover: hover) {
  html:root a.wip-row:hover { background-color: var(--wip-item-hover); }
  html:root a.wip-row:active { background-color: var(--wip-item-press); }
}
html:root a.wip-row:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--wip-ink-action); outline-offset: -2px; }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  html:root a.wip-row { transition: none; }
}
html:root:where([data-motion="reduce"]) a.wip-row { transition: none; }
/* The lines either side of a lit row give way, so no line runs into its
   round corners. The line above is the row's own. The line under it is
   drawn by the row a whole line of columns on, which is --wip-cols rows on:
   one, two from 640px and four from 1024px, the token's own breakpoints.
   Where a pointer hovers, as the ground is, so a row a tap left :hover on a
   phone does not lose its lines. */
@media (hover: hover) {
  html:root .wip-rows > li:has(> a.wip-row:is(:hover, :focus-visible)) { box-shadow: none; }
}
@media (hover: hover) and (max-width: 639.98px) {
  html:root .wip-rows-cols > li:has(> a.wip-row:is(:hover, :focus-visible)) + li { box-shadow: none; }
}
@media (hover: hover) and (min-width: 640px) and (max-width: 1023.98px) {
  html:root .wip-rows-cols > li:has(> a.wip-row:is(:hover, :focus-visible)) + li + li { box-shadow: none; }
}
@media (hover: hover) and (min-width: 1280px) {
  html:root .wip-rows-cols > li:has(> a.wip-row:is(:hover, :focus-visible)) + li + li + li + li { box-shadow: none; }
}
/* From 1024px to 1280px Home's lists are two across beside the open sidebar
   (BESIDE THE OPEN SIDEBAR, below), so there the line is two rows on. */
@media (hover: hover) and (min-width: 1024px) and (max-width: 1279.98px) {
  html:root:not(:has(> body > #platform-tabs:not(.hidden):not(.platform-tabs-route-hidden):not(.platform-tabs-folded))) .wip-rows-cols > li:has(> a.wip-row:is(:hover, :focus-visible)) + li + li + li + li { box-shadow: none; }
  html:root body:has(> #platform-tabs:not(.hidden):not(.platform-tabs-route-hidden):not(.platform-tabs-folded)) #home-screen .wip-rows-cols > li:has(> a.wip-row:is(:hover, :focus-visible)) + li + li { box-shadow: none; }
}
/* A panel: a card's inside, padded, for a list (Needs you). */
.wip-panel { padding: var(--wip-card-pad); min-width: 0; }
.wip-count {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  min-width: 1.5rem;
  height: 1.5rem;
  padding-inline: 0.375rem;
  border-radius: var(--wip-radius-small);
  background-color: var(--wip-count-bg);
  color: var(--wip-count-ink);
  font-size: var(--wip-body-size);
  font-weight: 700;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--wip-count-line);
}
.wip-item-icon {
  display: flex;
  flex: none;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: var(--wip-item-icon);
  height: var(--wip-item-icon);
  overflow: hidden;
  border-radius: var(--wip-item-icon-radius);
  object-fit: cover;
  font-size: 1.125rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--wip-ink-soft);
}
.wip-item-text { display: flex; flex: 1 1 0; flex-direction: column; min-width: 0; }
.wip-item-title {
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
  font-size: var(--wip-link-size);
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1.3;
  color: var(--wip-ink);
}
.wip-item-meta {
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
  font-size: var(--wip-body-size);
  line-height: 1.3;
  color: var(--wip-ink-muted);
}
/* A row's one pill: filled in the accent when it asks for the viewer. */
.wip-pill {
  flex: none;
  padding: 0.1875rem 0.625rem;
  border-radius: var(--wip-radius-small);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--wip-card-line);
  font-size: var(--wip-body-size);
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--wip-ink-soft);
}
.wip-pill-action {
  border-radius: var(--wip-radius-pill); /* it is what the row is pressed for */
  box-shadow: none;
  background-color: var(--wip-pill-bg);
  color: var(--wip-pill-ink);
}
/* Posters: things side by side, each a card of its own (the markup gives
   it the look's card, .wip-card), as wide as one of the look's columns and a
   card gap from the next, so the row's cards fall on the same lines as the
   cards above it. The row around them draws nothing. It scrolls sideways
   for what does not fit, by a swipe, a wheel or the arrow drawn at
   whichever end has more (Challenges). A poster is its picture at the side,
   on the picture's own tone, and its words beside it. */
/* The box the row and its arrows are placed in. It holds the row's margins
   (below), so its height is the cards' own and an arrow's 50% is their
   middle. */
.wip-posters-box { position: relative; display: flow-root; }
/* A scroller clips whatever its cards draw outside their boxes: the shadow
   under the pointer (8px to a side, 16px below) and the focus ring (4px).
   So the row reaches that far past the cards, half a card gap to a side,
   and its margins give the room back: the cards stay on the column's lines,
   a card scrolled just out of view is half a gap past the row's edge, where
   none of it shows, and a page of the row (an arrow's press) is exactly the
   cards on screen. */
.wip-posters {
  display: flex;
  gap: var(--wip-card-gap);
  margin-block: -0.25rem -1rem;
  margin-inline: calc(var(--wip-card-gap) / -2);
  padding-block: 0.25rem 1rem;
  padding-inline: calc(var(--wip-card-gap) / 2);
  overflow-x: auto;
  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
  scroll-padding-inline: calc(var(--wip-card-gap) / 2);
  scrollbar-width: none;
}
.wip-posters::-webkit-scrollbar { width: 0; height: 0; }
/* Inside, the card's own padding, and 4px more at its end: the row's arrow
   is drawn over that edge when there is more that way (below), and this
   keeps what the challenge is worth clear of it. */
.wip-poster {
  display: flex;
  flex: 0 0 calc((100% - (var(--wip-cols, 1) - 1) * var(--wip-card-gap)) / var(--wip-cols, 1));
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--wip-tile-gap);
  min-width: 0;
  padding: var(--wip-card-pad);
  padding-inline-end: calc(var(--wip-card-pad) + 0.25rem);
  text-align: start;
  scroll-snap-align: start;
  transition: box-shadow 0.45s cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.61, 0.36, 1);
}
/* Under the pointer, or reached by the keyboard, a card's edge deepens half
   a shade and a faint, wide shadow comes up under it, slowly, as a card on
   the Workshop board does; nothing moves. The keyboard's card also wears
   the page's focus ring, in its action colour. */
.wip-poster:is(:hover, :focus-visible) { box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--wip-card-line-hover), 0 8px 22px -14px var(--wip-card-line-hover); }
.wip-poster:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--wip-ink-action); outline-offset: 2px; }
.wip-poster:hover .wip-item-title { color: var(--wip-ink-action); }
.wip-poster-art {
  display: flex;
  flex: none;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: var(--wip-poster-art);
  height: var(--wip-poster-art);
  overflow: hidden;
  border-radius: var(--wip-icon-radius);
  font-size: 1.75rem;
  /* The picture's own tone (.home-tone-* sets --tone-50 and its deeper
     --tone-light-line) over the card, in the parts the tokens say: the pale
     tone in the light, a third of the deep one in the dark, where the
     shell's own dark tone is too near black to read as a colour. A
     hairline's grey without a tone. */
  background-color: color-mix(
    in srgb,
    var(--tone-50, var(--wip-card-line)) var(--wip-poster-tone),
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--tone-light-line, var(--wip-card-line)) var(--wip-poster-deep), var(--wip-card-bg))
  );
}
.wip-poster-art > img { width: 82%; height: 82%; object-fit: contain; transition: transform 200ms; }
.wip-poster:hover .wip-poster-art > img { transform: scale(1.06); }
/* The row's arrows: a disc over the edge of the card at that end of the
   row, at whichever end has more. */
.wip-rail-arrow {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: 2rem;
  height: 2rem;
  margin-top: -1rem;
  border-radius: var(--wip-radius-pill);
  background-color: var(--wip-card-bg);
  color: var(--wip-ink);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--wip-card-line), var(--wip-icon-shadow);
  transition: transform 150ms;
}
.wip-rail-arrow:hover { transform: scale(1.08); }
/* Centred on the card's edge; on a phone, whose gutter is 12px, a little
   further in, so all of the disc is on the screen. */
.wip-rail-arrow-back { inset-inline-start: -1rem; }
.wip-rail-arrow-on { inset-inline-end: -1rem; }
@media (max-width: 639.98px) {
  .wip-rail-arrow-back { inset-inline-start: -0.625rem; }
  .wip-rail-arrow-on { inset-inline-end: -0.625rem; }
}
/* Where a finger moves the row there are no arrows (phone review, 23 Nov
   2026): the disc hung half off the card, 2px from the screen's edge, over
   the points, and after a swipe a second one covered the first picture. The
   row swipes; the arrows are a pointer's way along it. */
@media (hover: none) and (pointer: coarse) {
  .wip-rail-arrow { display: none; }
}
.wip-rail-arrow-glyph { width: 1rem; height: 1rem; }
.wip-poster-facts { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.5rem; }
.wip-poster-facts > .wip-item-meta { flex: 1 1 0; min-width: 0; }
/* What it is worth is the line's strong half: a step larger than where the
   viewer stands, in the ink. Earned, it is in the done colour. */
.wip-poster-worth {
  flex: none;
  padding: 0.0625rem 0.4375rem;
  border-radius: var(--wip-radius-small);
  background-color: var(--wip-worth-bg);
  font-size: var(--wip-link-size);
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--wip-worth-ink);
}
.wip-poster-worth-ok { color: var(--wip-ok); }
/* A meter: a track, and the accent as far as the thing has come; green once
   it is done. */
.wip-meter {
  display: block;
  height: 0.375rem;
  margin-top: 0.5rem;
  overflow: hidden;
  border-radius: var(--wip-radius-pill);
  background-color: var(--wip-card-line);
}
.wip-meter > span { display: block; height: 100%; border-radius: inherit; background-color: var(--wip-meter); }
.wip-poster[data-state="done"] .wip-meter > span { background-color: var(--wip-ok); }
/* Before it is started there is nothing to measure: the meter keeps its
   place, so the posters stay level, and draws no track that would read as a
   rule under the words. */
.wip-poster[data-state="new"] .wip-meter { background-color: transparent; }
/* Placeholders: the shape of what is still on its way. Each is a block of
   the placeholder's fill; whoever holds them pulses. */
/* The fill falls back to the shell's hairline where a look borrows the kit without naming one. */
.wip-skel { display: block; flex: none; border-radius: var(--wip-radius-small); background-color: var(--wip-skeleton, var(--app-sheet-line)); }
.wip-skel-icon { width: var(--wip-item-icon); height: var(--wip-item-icon); border-radius: var(--wip-item-icon-radius); }
.wip-skel-lines { gap: 0.4375rem; }
.wip-skel-line { width: 78%; height: 0.6875rem; }
.wip-skel-line-short { width: 44%; height: 0.5625rem; }
.wip-skel-pill { width: 3rem; height: 1.5rem; border-radius: var(--wip-radius-pill); }
/* A card's placeholder: a picture's 16:10 and the three lines of words under it. */
.wip-skel-card { height: auto; padding-bottom: calc(62.5% + 4.5rem); border-radius: var(--wip-card-radius); }
/* RECENT ACTIVITY (features/home/needs-you-card.tsx, 24 Nov 2026): Home's
   row of project cards, one per project the viewer has work in, each the
   page's card (.wip-card) about 150px wide, so two and a bit show on a
   375px phone and the cut third says the row scrolls. The row runs to both
   edges of the screen and starts its first card on the page's gutter; it
   snaps to cards and draws no scrollbar. Where it has room for every card
   it does not scroll. As the posters' row does, it reaches past the cards
   for what a card draws outside its box (the shadow under the pointer, the
   focus ring), and its margins give that room back. */
.wip-activity-rail {
  display: flex;
  gap: 10px;
  margin-block: -0.25rem -1rem;
  margin-inline: calc(-1 * var(--wip-gutter, 12px));
  padding-block: 0.25rem 1rem;
  padding-inline: var(--wip-gutter, 12px);
  overflow-x: auto;
  overflow-y: hidden;
  overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
  scroll-snap-type: x proximity;
  scroll-padding-inline: var(--wip-gutter, 12px);
  scrollbar-width: none;
}
.wip-activity-rail::-webkit-scrollbar { width: 0; height: 0; }
.wip-activity-rail > li { display: flex; flex: none; scroll-snap-align: start; }
/* A card: its icon and the chevron at its top corner, its name and up to
   two lines of what is going on, then when it was last active at its foot.
   The words keep room for two lines whatever a card says, and the card a
   floor of that height, so every card is one height and a placeholder is
   the card it stands for. */
.wip-activity-card {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 10px;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  width: 150px;
  min-height: 9.75rem; /* 12 + 40 + 10 + 56 + 10 + 16 + 12 */
  padding: 12px;
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}
.wip-activity-top { display: flex; align-items: flex-start; justify-content: space-between; gap: 0.5rem; }
.wip-activity-words { display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-width: 0; min-height: 3.5rem; }
.wip-activity-when { margin-top: auto; }
.wip-activity-card .wip-skel-icon { width: 2.5rem; height: 2.5rem; border-radius: 1rem; }
.wip-activity-card .wip-skel-lines { padding-top: 0.25rem; }
/* Under the pointer, or reached by the keyboard, its edge deepens half a
   shade and a faint shadow comes up under it, slowly, as a challenge's
   poster does; nothing moves. Only where a pointer hovers: a tap on a phone
   can leave a card :hover. (The brand look keeps its own lift, so the fade
   is the standard look's.) */
html:root .wip-activity-card { transition: box-shadow 0.45s cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.61, 0.36, 1); }
@media (hover: hover) {
  .wip-activity-card[href]:hover { box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--wip-card-line-hover, var(--wip-card-line)), 0 8px 22px -14px var(--wip-card-line-hover, var(--wip-card-line)); }
  .wip-activity-card[href]:hover .wip-item-title { color: var(--wip-ink-action); }
}
.wip-activity-card:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--wip-ink-action); outline-offset: 2px; }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  html:root .wip-activity-card { transition: none; }
}
html:root:where([data-motion="reduce"]) .wip-activity-card { transition: none; }
/* A filter line: the choices at its start (they scroll sideways when they
   do not fit) and one menu at its end, close over the list they filter. */
.wip-filterbar {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--wip-card-gap);
  padding-bottom: 0.625rem;
}
/* Its menu is one of the line's own, at the line's end (`.wip-sort`, next). */
html:root .wip-filterbar > .wip-sort { margin-inline-start: auto; }
/* The sort menu, wherever it is drawn (the filter line, or a line of its own
   over a search's results): a chip's height, shape and type, on the card's
   colour under a hairline, with our own chevron at its end where the
   browser drew its arrow. It is a <select> still, so the open list is the
   browser's own; a select takes no ::after, so the chevron is an icon beside
   it that a press goes through. The signed-out page gives it the site's
   colours (THE WEBSITE'S LOOK ON THE SIGNED-OUT PAGES). */
.wip-sort {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-flex;
  flex: none;
  color: var(--wip-ink);
}
html:root .wip-sort > select {
  -webkit-appearance: none;
  appearance: none;
  width: auto;
  height: 2.75rem;
  padding-block: 0;
  padding-inline: 1.25rem 2.5rem;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 9999px;
  background-color: var(--wip-card-bg);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--wip-card-line);
  font-size: 0.9375rem;
  font-weight: 500;
  color: inherit;
  cursor: pointer;
}
html:root .wip-sort > select:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid currentColor; outline-offset: 2px; }
.wip-sort-mark {
  position: absolute;
  inset-inline-end: 1rem;
  top: 50%;
  width: 1rem;
  height: 1rem;
  margin-top: -0.5rem;
  pointer-events: none;
}
/* On a phone the menu takes a line of its own, under the choices (phone
   review, 23 Nov 2026): side by side, the menu left the choices about 217px,
   two of them fit and the third was cut to "So" against it. The choices get
   the whole line, and fade at the edge where there are more. Up to 480px:
   the folded Duo is 466 wide with 382 to use. */
@media (max-width: 479.98px) {
